#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 540.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125377} :activity/id #uuid "c72df862-0af9-5865-8585-d7236cb48cda" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048510 :topic/id #uuid "a58e9ae3-7402-5272-b80f-f01ca3bb8c44" :topic/math-academy-id 1893} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125377 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125774 :tutorial/content "Consider the quadric surface with the equation\n\n$$z = x^2 + y^2$$\n\nwhose graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/14/14e8d23a4bc649257c2f7351e0d2c1d62e39a8cd8eed1467e65853ee121b836e.png)\n\nThis surface is an example of an **elliptic paraboloid.**\n\nThe **vertex** of this elliptic paraboloid is located at the origin, and its **axis of symmetry** is the $z$-axis. Note that the axis of symmetry is the vertical line that passes through the vertex.\n\nMore generally, the equation of an elliptic paraboloid oriented along the $z$-axis whose vertex is at the point $(x_0,y_0,z_0)$ is given by\n\n$$z - z_0 = \\dfrac{(x-x_0)^2}{a^2} + \\dfrac{(y-y_0)^2}{b^2},$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants.\n\nThese surfaces are called *elliptic* *paraboloids* because their traces in the $xy$-plane are *ellipses*, while their traces in the $yz$- and $xz$-planes are *parabolas*. We'll discuss the traces of this surface in more detail shortly." :tutorial/id #uuid "d5da7d20-bcec-546d-8cd6-178215fc2096" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3439 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "0155d173-f619-5729-9721-6203bf768219" :step/math-academy-id 19459 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125378}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125378 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124504 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124313 :question/id #uuid "945f684f-9707-5a0a-b5d7-0753f11b4385" :question/math-academy-id "e-5266"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ddf82ff9-ea7c-5f7f-ac18-07f8e75b717e" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048350} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049336} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050991}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124896} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124931} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125218} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284456} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284457} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284458} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339072} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381300}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Properties of an Elliptic Paraboloid"} :step/id #uuid "32ea6077-6c22-5a3f-bce8-05b8b28c4283" :step/math-academy-id 19461 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125380}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125380 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125776 :tutorial/content "Now consider the graph of the quadric surface\n\n$$z = x^2 -y^2$$\n\nwhose graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/5f/5f02c207742c737d00cdeabbd2cbba93a88a42521c41a2cc1e9f19d27ff55f20.png)\n\nThis surface is an example of a **hyperbolic paraboloid** whose **saddle point** is at the origin.\n\nMore generally, a hyperbolic paraboloid whose saddle point is at $(x_0,y_0,z_0)$ has the general equation $$z - z_0 = \\dfrac{(x-x_0)^2}{a^2}- \\dfrac{(y-y_0)^2}{b^2}$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ constants.\n\nThis surface is called *hyperbolic* *paraboloid* because its traces in the $xy$-plane are *hyperbolas*, while its traces in the $yz$- and $xz$-planes are *parabolas*. We'll discuss the traces of this surface in more detail shortly.\n\nAnother property is that this surface mirrors itself across the planes $x=0$ and $y=0.$ Since these planes meet along the $z$-axis, that axis serves as the **axis of symmetry** of the hyperbolic paraboloid." :tutorial/id #uuid "15370709-5e87-5dcb-8a1f-ff66b0bf86e0" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3442 :tutorial/title "Hyperbolic Paraboloids"} :step/id #uuid "68121b3e-5396-564e-baa5-4f10972910c1" :step/math-academy-id 19465 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125381}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125381 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124505 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124314 :question/id #uuid "7e876c7b-849f-59ce-a654-881ec9464475" :question/math-academy-id "e-5269"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9078ab1-37a4-5e96-ae12-a83d5cd8804e" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048866} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051028}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124891} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124898} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284459} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284461} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339086} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339087} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339088} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342098} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342099} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366233} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382249}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Properties of a Hyperbolic Paraboloid"} :step/id #uuid "74d8bc9d-43f4-50df-82fd-204384b7d677" :step/math-academy-id 19466 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125461}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125460 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124556 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124365 :question/id #uuid "5ce5b329-ac5d-5581-b396-eadd569b9bec" :question/math-academy-id "e-6087"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dff86a8b-7966-53f7-a7bc-287990fb611d" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049118} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049699}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124897} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124923} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284464} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284465} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323729} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323730} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381910} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381935} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402441} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402475} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418046}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding Traces of Paraboloids"} :step/id #uuid "d6a4a4b3-6632-5593-890e-334f7d6d897e" :step/math-academy-id 22414} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125461 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125805 :tutorial/content "So far, we've encountered elliptic paraboloids whose axes of symmetry are parallel to the $z$-axis. Let's now consider the cases when an elliptic paraboloid is oriented along the $x$- or the $y$-axes.\n\nFor an elliptic paraboloid, the other two cases are as follows.\n\n- When the vertex is at the origin and the $y$ term is linear, the axis of symmetry is the $y$-axis: $$y = \\dfrac{x^2}{a^2} + \\dfrac{z^2}{c^2}$$\n\n![](images/da/da37415ddd27764a719fecc5780ee91e6e8ad3f5ce877101b81d1a955c57eb6f.png)\n- When the vertex is at the origin and the $x$ term is linear, the axis of symmetry is the $x$-axis: $$x = \\dfrac{y^2}{b^2} + \\dfrac{z^2}{c^2}$$\n\n![](images/d1/d1488d9f4767312795a84ce6441f64dd567223d1de4da66adbd6e39de9612a53.png)\n\nLikewise, for a hyperbolic paraboloid, the other two cases are as follows:\n\n- When the saddle point is at the origin and the $y$ term is linear, the axis of symmetry is the $y$-axis. The two corresponding equations are $$y = \\dfrac{x^2}{a^2} - \\dfrac{z^2}{c^2} \\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad y = \\dfrac{z^2}{c^2} - \\dfrac{x^2}{a^2}.$$\n- When the saddle point is at the origin and the $x$ term is linear, the axis of symmetry is the $x$-axis. The two corresponding equations are $$x = \\dfrac{z^2}{c^2} - \\dfrac{y^2}{b^2} \\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad x = \\dfrac{y^2}{b^2} - \\dfrac{z^2}{c^2}.$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "7f53539f-e8db-5205-b9d0-3919805097ae" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3851 :tutorial/title "Alternative Orientations of the Axis of Symmetry"} :step/id #uuid "26ce593b-6eaa-5a9c-a0cc-f0cf3649224d" :step/math-academy-id 22415 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125638}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125638 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125880 :tutorial/content "The traces of an elliptic paraboloid are either ellipses, parabolas, a single point, or the empty set.\n\nFor example, let's find the traces with the coordinate planes for the elliptic paraboloid given by\n\n$$z=x^2+y^2.$$\n\n- The trace of this surface in the $xy$-plane (when $z=0$) is given by the equation $$\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =0,\\end{aligned}$$ which corresponds to the origin $(0,0,0).$\n\n![](images/02/021dd988863021f564a0e85fb3db93030740059f2a50ade6504b3e12a5665372.png)\n\n- The trace of this surface in the $xz$-plane (when $y=0$) is given by the equation $$\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{0}^{2} & =z \\\\ {x}^{2} & =z,\\end{aligned}$$ which is a parabola in the $xz$-plane that has its vertex at the origin.\n\n![](images/9f/9f6cd0703b5d7b2458cbfd688d91f08a79752fa1ccd3616e5975f5c017aa7b42.png)\n\n- The trace of this surface in the $yz$-plane (when $x=0$) is given by the equation $$\\begin{aligned}{0}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =z \\\\ {y}^{2} & =z,\\end{aligned}$$ which is a parabola in the $yz$-plane that has its vertex at the origin.\n\n![](images/89/89be283ed121d3e30b1913e4412b8d3556ed611ef2a9a9bfd686d0594409f5c9.png)\n\nThere are a few other cases we should note:\n\n- The trace of our surface with the plane $z=-k$ for $k > 0$ is the empty set. This is because $z\\geq 0$ everywhere on the elliptic paraboloid.\n- The trace of our surface with the plane $z=k$ for $k > 0$ is an ellipse: $$x^2+y^2 = k$$\n\n![](images/f1/f15e925431b0d2ccfd4afa703cad1bcb37b65a75e96fbeabcf4823703bf184e5.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "0ea2e49f-f5aa-57b1-9b67-de6947a0e6e5" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7839 :tutorial/title "Traces of Elliptic Paraboloids"} :step/id #uuid "587634e3-d3a5-57e5-bd78-93b3ed921e5b" :step/math-academy-id 45282 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125639}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125639 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125881 :tutorial/content "The traces of a hyperbolic paraboloid are either hyperbolas, parabolas, or intersecting lines.\n\nFor example, let's find the traces with the coordinate planes for the hyperbolic paraboloid given by\n\n$$z=x^2-y^2.$$\n\n- The trace of this surface in the $xy$-plane (when $z=0$) is given by the equation $$\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-{y}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2} & ={x}^{2} \\\\ y & =\\pm x\\end{aligned}$$ which corresponds to two intersecting lines in the $xy$-plane.\n\n![](images/9b/9b3fbb9d8945c5d9208a59d404e489e4a339082c602c18154010e3e53083b220.png)\n\n- The trace of this surface in the $xz$-plane (when $y=0$) is given by the equation $$\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-{0}^{2} & =z \\\\ z & ={x}^{2},\\end{aligned}$$ which corresponds to the parabola in the $xz$-plane that has its vertex at the origin.\n\n![](images/e8/e8e2b6306052e781fd149e4eebb04da79eddb44b5261b05b129f208ad523b70b.png)\n\n- The trace of this surface in the $yz$-plane (when $x=0$) is given by the equation $$\\begin{aligned}{0}^{2}-{y}^{2} & =z \\\\ z & =-{y}^{2},\\end{aligned}$$ which corresponds to the parabola in the $yz$-plane that has its vertex at the origin.\n\n![](images/70/7010e4c26af60c6c27f66c93b805a2bac1e191c1646b5aac3a77f5dd3dadc64f.png)\n\nIn this particular case, the **saddle point** is at the origin. It is a minimum point for the trace in the $xz$-plane but a maximum point for the trace in the $yz$-plane.\n\nFinally, note that the trace of our surface with the plane $z=k\\neq 0$ is a hyperbola: $$x^2-y^2 = k$$\n\n![](images/b9/b9c1baab2f9d85c2c415cae91908d5c908aa0b1cef1eb8b5901f7f3b78bf66b2.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "c2467f92-856a-5690-bd8d-1c593268396a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7840 :tutorial/title "Traces of Hyperbolic Paraboloids"} :step/id #uuid "d9e46e54-59aa-5284-ac11-65d16e6bca74" :step/math-academy-id 45283 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125460}}] :activity/title "Paraboloids" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124696}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418049} :activity/id #uuid "58642185-16fa-5318-8c89-f9d4ccf0c829" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048510 :topic/id #uuid "a58e9ae3-7402-5272-b80f-f01ca3bb8c44" :topic/math-academy-id 1893} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418047 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284465 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "297fea90-37a9-5380-b47a-81c1e6d84465" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\sqrt{3}$ and $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282051} {:answer/id #uuid "3704ecec-4aab-5a7a-bf09-0c5c3fc66880" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$2\\sqrt{2}$ and $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282178} {:answer/id #uuid "8c4ec860-b6b0-5930-9174-508c8b04299f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$9$ and $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282903} {:answer/id #uuid "8dd3abf6-da14-5f2d-a3c4-85c8c1e8823a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\sqrt{3}$ and $\\sqrt{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282918} {:answer/id #uuid "ccccbae0-09b3-5285-928f-468dfef6fcee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$3$ and $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283478}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186282918} :answer-field/id #uuid "858af6d8-4d33-5e09-9265-e6425c3e36a2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284161}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c4fbc092-8a05-5e53-ba3e-bf69c3dd36b6" :question/math-academy-id "q-164282" :question/problem "The projection of the intersection between the elliptic paraboloid $4(x-5)^{2}+6(y+4)^{2}=z+8$ and the plane \n$z=4$  onto the $xy$-plane is an ellipse. What are the lengths of the semi-axes of the ellipse?" :question/worked-solution "To find the intersection of the paraboloid and the plane $z=4$, we substitute $z=4$ into the equation of the paraboloid, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(x-5)^{2}+6(y+4)^{2}=z+8 \\\\ 4(x-5)^{2}+6(y+4)^{2}=4+8 \\\\ 4(x-5)^{2}+6(y+4)^{2}=12 \\\\ \\frac{(x-5)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{(y+4)^{2}}{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-5)^{2}}{(\\sqrt{3})^{2}}+\\frac{(y+4)^{2}}{(\\sqrt{2})^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the equation of an ellipse with semi-axes of lengths $\\sqrt{3}$ and $\\sqrt{2}.$"} :step/id #uuid "af8eee1b-35b6-51ee-8480-e799f2ebb8d2"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418048 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339088 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4c67b3b1-ca17-50d9-826b-aa84c548da4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(-7,0,0)$ and $(1,0,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336743} {:answer/id #uuid "767a3d83-a217-545e-829c-803f0a2e5f93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(1,0,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337098} {:answer/id #uuid "7d9dcfbc-5db8-52dc-aa70-3ce3548a031a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(-7,0,0)$ and $(-1,0,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337168} {:answer/id #uuid "cd3c3792-dfb7-53e4-8d4f-f13a6930b54d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(-7,0,0)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337832} {:answer/id #uuid "fb10441f-5dc2-53c4-8a56-253eb94e9ba8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(-1,0,0)$ and $(7,0,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338235}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186336743} :answer-field/id #uuid "5a0d88c8-d602-5247-ade9-89205172c789" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338432}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "0b363aae-9a1a-5bd8-a7ce-6affaf56df43" :question/math-academy-id "q-72305" :question/problem "Given the hyperbolic paraboloid $(x+3)^{2}-2(y-3)^{2}=z-2$, find its intercepts with the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "To find the $x$-intercepts of the paraboloid, we substitute $y=0$ and $z=0$ in the equation of the paraboloid:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+3)^{2}-2(0-3)^{2} & =0-2 \\\\ (x+3)^{2}-18 & =-2 \\\\ (x+3)^{2} & =16 \\\\ x+3 & =\\pm 4 \\\\ x & =-3\\pm 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the solutions $x=-7$ and $x=1.$\n\nTherefore, the intercepts with the $x$-axis are $(-7,0,0)$ and $(1,0,0).$"} :step/id #uuid "b0e9f4df-c7d1-5c3e-9d66-792c898f4bac" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418047}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418049 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418046 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "019726e7-4913-502a-a919-de71561128cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\sqrt{3}$ and $\\sqrt{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418040} {:answer/id #uuid "31103cb3-8db8-5966-8f8f-150d81076c20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$3$ and $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418041} {:answer/id #uuid "3a2b823c-f5c0-585b-b3ff-0eec4bcfc2e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\sqrt{3}$ and $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418042} {:answer/id #uuid "9e786335-093c-5020-9d1b-5216151e0ab3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$3$ and $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418043} {:answer/id #uuid "ce083963-6a81-5daf-90c1-ac994c70d5be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\sqrt{3}$ and $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418044}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418042} :answer-field/id #uuid "8a4ae1c8-63c4-5962-bc3f-7d5f1634e01e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418045}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "5ea54c52-c613-575d-9b4a-ae98c73f35ea" :question/math-academy-id "q-35926" :question/problem "The projection of the intersection between the hyperbolic paraboloid $12z=4(x+2)^{2}-3{y}^{2}$ and the plane \n$z=1$  onto the $xy$-plane is a hyperbola. What are the lengths of the semi-axes of the hyperbola?" :question/worked-solution "To find the intersection of the paraboloid and the plane $z=1$, we substitute $z=1$ into the equation of the paraboloid, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12z & =4(x+2)^{2}-3{y}^{2} \\\\ 12(1) & =4(x+2)^{2}-3{y}^{2} \\\\ 12 & =4(x+2)^{2}-3{y}^{2} \\\\ 1 & =\\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{3}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} \\\\ 1 & =\\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{(\\sqrt{3})^{2}}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{2}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the equation of a hyperbola with semi-axes of lengths $\\sqrt{3}$ and $2.$"} :step/id #uuid "bbc73c40-2938-59e4-adf6-e2670e949f1e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418051}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418050 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366233 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7b4441e7-9ebc-5545-9efa-2ee1440fa1b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(0,0,0)$ and $(0,4,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366235} {:answer/id #uuid "6bc6b114-2ad4-5286-98ef-bab6af5d1a58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(0,0,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366236} {:answer/id #uuid "2b746e61-be9d-59cb-8bf8-3b68b34626f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(0,-4,0)$ and $(0,0,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366237} {:answer/id #uuid "e590b348-2055-5203-8c91-c618c41626da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(0,-4,0)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366238} {:answer/id #uuid "84bf4e2c-448d-5b40-8003-6a09149d5ddd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(0,-2,0)$ and $(0,0,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366239}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366237} :answer-field/id #uuid "98778e41-d388-55c7-822d-6a1e70584ae6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366234}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "16658c9e-8fa3-5c30-87d5-30c0a6e7db83" :question/math-academy-id "q-72302" :question/problem "Given the hyperbolic paraboloid $(x-6)^{2}-8(y+2)^{2}=z+4$, find its intercepts with the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "To find the $y$-intercepts of the paraboloid, we substitute $x=0$ and $z=0$ in the equation of the paraboloid:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0-6)^{2}-8(y+2)^{2} & =0+4 \\\\ 36-8(y+2)^{2} & =4 \\\\ 32 & =8(y+2)^{2} \\\\ (y+2)^{2} & =4 \\\\ y+2 & =\\pm 2 \\\\ y & =-2\\pm 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the solutions $y=-4$ and $y=0.$\n\nTherefore, the intercepts with the $y$-axis are $(0,-4,0)$ and $(0,0,0).$"} :step/id #uuid "c8e375d5-a259-54e0-ae9f-f99977167c51" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418048}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418051 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323729 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "205d1a65-04f6-59a3-a974-5b778afaa91e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321391} {:answer/id #uuid "4dcfa493-b877-5976-8c32-70d3d57ac828" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(-2,3)$ in the $xy$-plane" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321760} {:answer/id #uuid "5f6e9f3f-adaa-5df3-bb70-cd9f70742907" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(-2,-3)$ in the $xy$-plane" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321898} {:answer/id #uuid "e24be124-29de-5243-8c6e-8306d6f59e5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(2,-3)$ in the $xy$-plane" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322915} {:answer/id #uuid "eccdcbac-973f-5f39-80bc-76abea742d02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(2,3)$ in the $xy$-plane" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323016}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321898} :answer-field/id #uuid "e2f3b7e6-356f-5e12-ac50-e4c8413a78be" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323618}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "04c0f5ae-99dd-5b51-8d08-843351e49a3d" :question/math-academy-id "q-35930" :question/problem "The projection of the intersection between the elliptic paraboloid $5(x+2)^{2}+3(y+3)^{2}=z$ and the plane $z=2$  onto the $xy$-plane is an ellipse. Find the center of this ellipse." :question/worked-solution "To find the intersection of the paraboloid and the plane $z=2$, we substitute $z=2$ into the equation of the paraboloid, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5(x+2)^{2}+3(y+3)^{2} & =z \\\\ 5(x+2)^{2}+3(y+3)^{2} & =2 \\\\ \\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{2/5}+\\frac{(y+3)^{2}}{2/3} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the equation of an ellipse in the $xy$-plane that has its center at $(-2,-3).$"} :step/id #uuid "f747c054-c600-575d-8af5-3ac0554fc78c" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418050}}] :activity/title "Paraboloids" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418039}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172985} :activity/id #uuid "1bda195a-6aa9-5403-8c13-a26311503b1d" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048917 :topic/id #uuid "cc0e7551-dafb-5e86-9c0e-20d3207eccdf" :topic/math-academy-id 3344} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172983 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172083 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171889 :question/id #uuid "f0111439-4fd8-5add-b95f-5e783178fd63" :question/math-academy-id "e-9440"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5b00f0c-fc7f-5262-b1a3-cdd6c1560cd6" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050412}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172275} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172286} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256709} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256710} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256712} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380712} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387277} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387284} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387291} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417343}] :knowledge-point/title "Sketching a Two-Dimensional Vector Field"} :step/id #uuid "8de1d301-7c80-5374-bd47-c7e3b3821626" :step/math-academy-id 35366 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172990}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172984 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172084 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171890 :question/id #uuid "8f9615ef-b53e-51ee-9aa6-4df88afd6773" :question/math-academy-id "e-9441"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a9734fc0-9789-5a9c-8ae0-806f87052875" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172276} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172277} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256679} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256680} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256681} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256682} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256683} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256684} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380650}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Two-Dimensional Vector Field"} :step/id #uuid "7533639d-bfe8-58f0-b64f-5c767246da78" :step/math-academy-id 35367 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172989}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172985 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173197 :tutorial/content "A vector field on $\\mathbb R^2$ assigns a vector to every point $(x,y)$ in a region $S\\subset \\mathbb R^2$. We can visualize this by plotting the corresponding vector at each point in the coordinate plane.\n\nAs an example, let's sketch the vector field $\\mathbf F$ at the points $(1,1), (-3,3),$ and $(4,-3),$ where $\\mathbf F$ is given by\n\n$$\\mathbf F(x,y) = x \\, \\mathbf{i} + y \\, \\mathbf{j}.$$\n\nTo sketch the vector field at the given points, we first substitute their coordinates into the function $\\mathbf F\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}F(1,1) & =i+j \\\\ F(-3,3) & =-3\\,i+3\\,j \\\\ F(4,-3) & =4\\,i-3\\,j\\end{aligned}$$\n\nWe now draw our vectors, placing the tail ends of each vector at its corresponding point. This gives the following diagram:\n\n![](images/da/daea999f1a9bff587f0550e6674a39ae27dc9a2bb58e35877ed15626de0d9791.png)\n\nApplying the same method to more points in $\\mathbb{R}^2,$ we obtain the following visualization of our vector field:\n\n![](images/03/03402a7a7e1967b428c3657d6be90e4b1fd7ddd3c8ccaa11a69b0ff7cd566859.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "6ed5e3d1-f4e2-5773-bbd0-5ef02d8b726d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5763 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "f845bde7-8e1b-5e89-8f3e-66a8c7e912eb" :step/math-academy-id 35369 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172983}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172989 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172088 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171894 :question/id #uuid "b226c391-9538-59ff-85ff-dde4d73f209b" :question/math-academy-id "e-9473"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "888aa30d-7179-507c-a30c-38209587333b" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172282} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172284} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256686} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256689} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256690} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259551} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259552} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259553} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371665} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375804}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Three-Dimensional Vector Field"} :step/id #uuid "64cb83b5-85c0-5f79-8253-5fd52adedcd8" :step/math-academy-id 35450} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172990 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173198 :tutorial/content "Given a visualization, it's possible to determine characteristics about the underlying vector field.\n\nFor example, let's consider the visualization below.\n\n![](images/6e/6e3ecb8b2d64aef1cf4654fea8e3ad8f176e9d830ee8b402a281155c3b4ef645.png)\n\nThis visualization was generated by one of the following functions.\n\n1. $\\mathbf F(x,y) = \\mathbf{i} - \\mathbf{j}$\n2. $\\mathbf G(x,y) = x \\, \\mathbf{i} + \\mathbf{j}$\n3. $\\mathbf H(x,y) = y \\, \\mathbf{i} + \\mathbf{j}$\n\nWe can determine which function generated the above diagram using a process of elimination:\n\n- The vertical component (i.e., the $\\mathbf j$ component) is positive for *every* vector in our vector field. This means we can exclude any function whose $\\mathbf j$ component is negative for some $(x,y) \\in \\mathbb{R}^2.$ Therefore, we can rule out the following option: $$\\mathbf F(x,y) = \\mathbf{i} - \\mathbf{j}$$\n- As we move away from the origin in the horizontal direction, our vectors' horizontal components (i.e., the $\\mathbf i$ components) get longer. This means that the $\\mathbf i$ component of our vector field is dependent on the variable $x.$\n\nSo, we can exclude any function whose $\\mathbf i$ component is independent of $x.$ Hence, we can rule out the following option: $$\\mathbf H(x,y) = y\\, \\mathbf{i} + \\mathbf{j}$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the diagram corresponds to the vector field $\\mathbf G(x,y) = x \\, \\mathbf{i} + \\mathbf{j}.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "372dc560-67d1-5888-9353-8f8f365cff15" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5773 :tutorial/title "Analyzing Vector Field Diagrams"} :step/id #uuid "51e7b312-b65a-5a9c-88d2-2bb1e9411b96" :step/math-academy-id 35467 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172984}}] :activity/title "Visualizing Vector Fields" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186172171}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417344} :activity/id #uuid "6f1cb101-0015-5868-a6fa-64d8ac5ce0c8" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048917 :topic/id #uuid "cc0e7551-dafb-5e86-9c0e-20d3207eccdf" :topic/math-academy-id 3344} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417344 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172282 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0af393b5-0b0a-51ee-963e-9232b624b8d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "G(x,y,z)=-i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253755} {:answer/id #uuid "4ebe45a6-6f14-5783-a2c5-0fff74a623ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "K(x,y,z)={e}^{x}\\,i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254352} {:answer/id #uuid "5a235ec0-b137-57da-bcd7-fa7e7f95a670" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "H(x,y,z)=i+j+k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254460} {:answer/id #uuid "6c27c496-3691-52cb-89b6-a510f5ec6427" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "F(x,y,z)=i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254631} {:answer/id #uuid "99ad8cef-0db5-5109-bbb4-eb38f07c8dc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255028}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186254631} :answer-field/id #uuid "1226930a-6b24-57fb-a5e7-1829039d9728" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255946}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "505eb075-afb5-5225-b846-447773bcc89d" :question/math-academy-id "q-122559" :question/problem "![](images/cf/cfc15b6202167211b7d6c0e8f6635dd9e571810d5296a209a4c317350604c4a4.png)\n\nWhich of the following could be the function that generates the vector field above?" :question/worked-solution "We notice the following:\n\n- Each vector in our vector field is parallel to the $x$-axis. So, the $j$ and $k$ components of our vector field are zero everywhere. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nH(x,y,z)=i+j+k,\\,\n$$\n\n- Moreover, each vector in our vector field is pointing in the *positive* $x$-direction, so the $i$ component of our vector field is positive. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nG(x,y,z)=-i\n$$\n\n- Finally, we note that each vector in our vector field has the same length, so the $i$ component of our vector field is constant. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nK(x,y,z)={e}^{x}\\,i\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the diagram corresponds to the vector field $F(x,y,z)=i.$"} :step/id #uuid "0dc29e20-9d3a-5e3d-9c90-7b8b29d2ac4f" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417346}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417345 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417343 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "49e846d3-cfa4-53b9-966f-e4adab0cb729" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/1d/1d502f64bd89571fcd7ccb43e339b0fb8554ebf2072d48eaf10829f96f9c170e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417331} {:answer/id #uuid "acf72fee-ab09-5c2f-a567-2d99703c6cb0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/ea/eac1fe8c8dee7394a49603187829adcc0fa81665e0eafb2674f43ebdf4493f10.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417334} {:answer/id #uuid "b10e879c-3c4a-5dd0-9361-bbc17fe37c13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/5d/5d27f4544c601da995ad2c1402547a477a244a6b79bff4e8b79f945299b73b87.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417335} {:answer/id #uuid "cf5ff7a9-5c75-54e7-82a4-5ae809e4f3a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/e4/e40599ebb674fe5fe05c60be75f148f41458469ae6926c3388675ecdb24f66cf.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417337} {:answer/id #uuid "d640a56e-f8d2-517d-a8ab-e60c9f56c036" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/59/59bf689a32a8a0c5d347f29065acea60194b9f4b0fad084108ecb657257cd939.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417338}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417337} :answer-field/id #uuid "b10e9eb4-ee70-5020-8593-2fc167326803" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417341}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d04f0b91-140d-549f-8f9d-31d1d9b6c7bc" :question/math-academy-id "q-122758" :question/problem "Which of the following diagrams represents the vector field $F(x,y)=-x\\,i-y\\,j$ at the indicated points?" :question/worked-solution "We have to sketch the vector field at the points $(1,4),(2,-2),$ and $(4,-2).$\n\nTo sketch the vector field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the function $F(x,y)=-x\\,i-y\\,j\\,:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(1,4) & =-(1)\\,i-4\\,j=-i-4\\,j \\\\ F(2,-2) & =-(2)\\,i-(-2)\\,j=-2\\,i+2\\,j \\\\ F(4,-2) & =-(4)\\,i-(-2)\\,j=-4\\,i+2\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now draw our vectors. At each point, we draw the vector associated with that point, placing the tail end of the vector on the point itself. This gives the following diagram.\n\n![](images/e4/e40599ebb674fe5fe05c60be75f148f41458469ae6926c3388675ecdb24f66cf.png)"} :step/id #uuid "1b8d0142-f4e6-56fc-84f8-a8543a949309" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417347}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417346 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256681 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ba98b8c-2116-50e2-b2de-6aae7dbae22c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "K(x,y)=-i-2\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254058} {:answer/id #uuid "735ac856-ee4b-5c8a-9daf-474103fa33a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "H(x,y)=x\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254692} {:answer/id #uuid "a2214b75-4ff9-5986-b2e2-09f3f7eea2eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255100} {:answer/id #uuid "e51e621f-adf6-56d4-ba3a-23552d243dc2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "G(x,y)=5\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255694} {:answer/id #uuid "fce86be7-59a1-5b1f-98d2-ae97d81427f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "F(x,y)=i+j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255891}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186254692} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a1d0012-9007-5d62-a389-7be8930ea3d3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256203}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b5e48f80-38d5-59fb-be5e-a4668cd85716" :question/math-academy-id "q-122632" :question/problem "![](images/a0/a032eb49f78ce7bd54de6969f030e4316f2bd3028280f73991af3fe45521733c.png)\n\nWhich of the following could be the function that generates the vector field above?" :question/worked-solution "We notice the following:\n\n- The horizontal component (i.e., the $i$ component) is zero for *every* vector in our vector field. Therefore, we can rule out the following options:\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=i+j,\\,K(x,y)=-i-2\\,j\n$$\n\n- As we move away from the origin in the horizontal direction, our vectors' vertical components (i.e., the $j$ components) get longer. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nG(x,y)=5\\,j\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the diagram corresponds to the vector field $H(x,y)=x\\,j.$"} :step/id #uuid "5abc9e5b-dfec-5521-ae1a-20eaaff9c78a" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417348}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417347 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259551 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "44832c8e-8988-5ee0-ac8b-67ae439d923f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257381} {:answer/id #uuid "70a0b147-68a3-5ec0-ae90-b9c82057aad3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "H(x,y,z)=-2\\,i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257773} {:answer/id #uuid "96d0c94c-faa8-5d39-b3c3-d13382b0eced" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "F(x,y,z)=i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258114} {:answer/id #uuid "acdf99e3-cf16-5362-88ce-860873156446" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "K(x,y,z)=-i+j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258289} {:answer/id #uuid "f8d361e0-0169-5758-b933-86a6b5b677fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "G(x,y,z)=-y\\,i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258965}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257773} :answer-field/id #uuid "861c8be9-902d-5545-831a-501704ab8be1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259264}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "1c63175a-a455-54b8-b784-f9c30e4507e1" :question/math-academy-id "q-123748" :question/problem "![](images/bf/bff60466ca8abba4fd849c046c02af3a4811279774975fc981cafad91d734ed5.png)\n\nWhich of the following could be the function that generates the vector field above?" :question/worked-solution "We notice the following:\n\n- Each vector in our vector field is parallel to the $x$-axis. So, the $j$ and $k$ components of our vector field are zero everywhere. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nK(x,y,z)=-i+j\n$$\n\n- Moreover, each vector in our vector field is pointing in the *negative* $x$-direction, so the $i$ component of our vector field is negative. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=i\n$$\n\n- Finally, we note that each vector in our vector field has the same length, so the $i$ component of our vector field is constant. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nG(x,y,z)=-y\\,i\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the diagram corresponds to the vector field $H(x,y,z)=-2\\,i.$"} :step/id #uuid "6b4f8a97-87e5-50b3-a283-1ef6e69a5e50"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417348 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256690 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "52540afe-6bae-5db6-95a2-a9867a8f858b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417332} {:answer/id #uuid "52693f37-1a7d-5506-8ee4-6500aa2d7349" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "F(x,y,z)=i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417333} {:answer/id #uuid "c2ec2c00-c765-5813-aaa4-789423b68551" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "H(x,y,z)=k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417336} {:answer/id #uuid "f6c9d1db-461c-5c21-9d19-a6e9f1533a90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "G(x,y,z)=-j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417339} {:answer/id #uuid "ff5ef832-2edf-5758-ba7c-c1096ad3a8c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "K(x,y,z)=-{x}^{2}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417340}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417332} :answer-field/id #uuid "f30ffaea-faee-50f8-bb72-c0bdccf87c9a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417342}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "548bbae5-603d-5bc6-9f7d-4ca6dbc971eb" :question/math-academy-id "q-122670" :question/problem "![](images/64/64f02fa7dcd23b20e61801c0453d8fe7ed013936572e1ee63f748361edd4dec5.png)\n\nWhich of the following could be the function that generates the vector field above?" :question/worked-solution "We notice the following:\n\n- Each vector in our vector field is parallel to the $z$-axis. So, the $i$ and $j$ components of our vector field are zero everywhere. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=i,\\,G(x,y,z)=-j\n$$\n\n- Moreover, each vector in our vector field is pointing in the *negative* $z$-direction, so the $k$ component of our vector field is negative. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nH(x,y,z)=k\n$$\n\n- Finally, we note that each vector in our vector field has the same length, so the $k$ component of our vector field is constant. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nK(x,y,z)=-{x}^{2}\\,k\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the diagram doesn't correspond to any of the listed vector fields."} :step/id #uuid "80d8fd6a-6a07-5ff4-8ccd-33cb7f7a2af9" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417345}}] :activity/title "Visualizing Vector Fields" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417330}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 840.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079512} :activity/id #uuid "580243c0-444d-51d9-820f-ab39d2e434fb" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049375 :topic/id #uuid "eff38647-ee17-5513-ad67-94525b17bd40" :topic/math-academy-id 621} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079512 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079690 :tutorial/content "Consider the function $y=f(x)$ below. Suppose we know that $f(2)=5$ and $f'(2)=1.$ Based on this information, how can we approximate the value of $f(2.75)?$\n\n![](images/25/25baabc5d4193a480d09370faa104d34edc14b2aa09759f5976d592a387fb398.png)\n\nFirst, recall that the tangent line to $y=f(x)$ at $x=a$ is given by\n\n$$\ny-f(a) = f'(a)(x-a).\n$$\n\nSolving for $y$ in the above, we get \n\n$$\ny= f(a)+ f'(a)(x-a)\n$$\n\nNow, provided that $x$ is close to $a,$ we can assume that $y\\approx f(x) ,$ which gives us the approximation\n\n$$\nf(x)\\approx f(a)+ f'(a)(x-a).\n$$\n\nWe often represent the tangent line approximation using the notation $L(x),$ as follows:\n\n$$\nL(x)= f(a)+ f'(a)(x-a)\n$$\n\nIn this case, we want the tangent line approximation at $a=2.$ Substituting this value along with the given information, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =f(2)+{f}^{′}(2)(x-2) \\\\  & =(5)+(1)(x-2) \\\\  & =x+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's take a look at the graphs. Notice that in the neighborhood of $x=2$, the function and its tangent line approximation are very close. The line $L(x)$ is a good approximation of the function for points close to $x=2.$\n\n![](images/c1/c1de31c316c019e8faf466f64a0e6ff24a29935a7ec354c2acdf18d3069b8d47.png)\n\nAs we can see, ${\\color{blue}L(2.75)}$ and ${\\color{red}f(2.75)}$ are quite close. So, we can approximate $f(2.75)$ using $L(2.75)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2.75) & ≈L(2.75) \\\\  & =(2.75)+3 \\\\  & =5.75.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe tangent line approximation\n\n$$\nL(x)= f(a)+ f'(a)(x-a)\n$$\n\nis sometimes also called the **linear approximation** of the function $f(x)$ at the point $x=a.$ The process of approximating the function by its tangent is called **linearization.**" :tutorial/id #uuid "1fd16cbf-e232-56c9-84ba-d6b6ff2d147a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 579 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "80d363a5-ade6-554f-b554-78362a2a4a87" :step/math-academy-id 4825 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079513}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079513 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078566 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078231 :question/id #uuid "aea27069-47b4-50b0-9e70-3a65951916b8" :question/math-academy-id "e-291"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b08650da-89e7-5a9d-89a6-de3623344662" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050644}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078976} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078980} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297412} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319050} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319051} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319052} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319054} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363701} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364093} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364233} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364240} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364261} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432882} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432883} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432884}] :knowledge-point/title "Estimating the Value of a Function at a Point Using a Tangent Line Approximation"} :step/id #uuid "29daedd8-9104-56cf-8960-bc6517ab9536" :step/math-academy-id 4826 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079514}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079514 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078567 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078232 :question/id #uuid "44510fee-857c-5182-8f9e-a08c18966142" :question/math-academy-id "e-292"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b0dd714-5bf1-573f-9ff9-11802622ce92" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048191}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078972} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078975} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250433} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250437} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297413} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297415} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319057} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329871} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329909} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362811} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362887} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362894} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364226} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364392} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432886} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432888} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432889}] :knowledge-point/title "Estimating the Value of a Function at a Point Given its Derivative"} :step/id #uuid "0f65d689-6eed-51f9-99e7-de9d4bff5796" :step/math-academy-id 4829 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079515}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079515 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079691 :tutorial/content "Let $L(x)$ be the tangent line approximation of $y = f(x)$ at $x=a,$ as shown in the pictures below. \n\n| ![](images/ad/ad7375c544eed881d96d07b449d9003c7df813bdddcdaf29e82bfda6bd4cb283.png) | ![](images/b5/b5b3cd71da3243a5848021831403adde07f1487ff1077f3359d2564ba0f4b686.png) |\n| --- | --- |\n\nWe can make the following conclusions:\n\n- When the function is *concave up*, the tangent line is *below* the graph (see the picture on the left). Then, \n\n$$\nL(x) \\lt f(x)\n$$\n\nnear $x=a,$ and we say that $L(x)$ gives an *underestimate* of the function.\n\n- When the function is *concave down*, the tangent line is *above* the graph (see the picture on the right). Then, \n\n$$\nL(x) \\gt f(x)\n$$\n\nnear $x=a,$ and we say that $L(x)$ gives an *overestimate* of the function." :tutorial/id #uuid "717ddca8-0e12-5d53-9384-635de0a676ce" :tutorial/math-academy-id 582 :tutorial/title "Underestimate and Overestimate Approximations"} :step/id #uuid "539a8b72-5dd7-5bd3-b6ff-ecd4fb4535ed" :step/math-academy-id 4832 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079516}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079516 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078568 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078236 :question/id #uuid "dc311e80-fd79-5b89-9f0e-0b1caef6457e" :question/math-academy-id "e-293"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9f542453-9758-55e6-93c6-6bfd2139ec45" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049011}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078967} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078997} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250376} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297416} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319134} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364102} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364247} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364254} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397723} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399857} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399878} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402957} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405009} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432881} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432885} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432887}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Tangent Line Approximation and Classifying it as an Underestimate or Overestimate"} :step/id #uuid "0a4e1771-2f14-58d7-9a7d-62eb186154dd" :step/math-academy-id 4833}] :activity/title "Approximating Functions Using Local Linearity and Linearization" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186078647}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162847} :activity/id #uuid "f83b8c51-727f-5eb0-9f6f-bcc3b133cfd6" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050021 :topic/id #uuid "268943d4-aaa4-5e39-9db5-48d7523acdc3" :topic/math-academy-id 3118} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162847 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163222 :tutorial/content "A square matrix $A$ is **symmetric** if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA^T = A\n$$\n\nEquivalently, an $n\\times n$ matrix is symmetric if $a_{ij} = a_{ji}$ for all $1 \\leq i,j\\leq n.$\n\nIf $A$ is symmetric, this really means that it is symmetric across its main diagonal. \n\nFor example, the matrix \n\n![](images/0f/0f62a89f8da1ab7bfb86d46fddb441e95e5e3a9554e5a88662691b4df40e19af.svg)\n\nis symmetric since $A^T = A.$ \n\nOn the other hand, the matrix \n\n![](images/78/780de3c3f66b1037e4152224f4aa98b65b8b1b859adfe7c3881bb830d7eecd66.svg)\n\nis not symmetric. Indeed, since $b_{12} \\neq b_{21},$ we have $B^T \\neq B.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "1fe9d8bc-6133-5ab4-8b4f-af2b40817e02" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4880 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "9c19c64f-0597-5273-8ff3-50729d7a67fc" :step/math-academy-id 30531 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162859}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162859 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161979 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161771 :question/id #uuid "c68d70a2-e4f8-5f08-8220-3df420a1e199" :question/math-academy-id "e-8156"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c2da07e-23b5-58d0-aeef-14e21b329368" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050727}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162586} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162613} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272114} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275133} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275134} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357611} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357612} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357613} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366553} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366560} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425154} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490264} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490309} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490315}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Symmetric Matrices"} :step/id #uuid "f2c61bc7-19cc-5e6a-a533-0450e49aa5dd" :step/math-academy-id 30550 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162924}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162924 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163254 :tutorial/content "Suppose that $A$ is a symmetric matrix. Then, we have the following properties:\n\n- $-A$ is symmetric\n\n- $A^m$ is symmetric for any positive integer $m$\n\n- $A^{-1}$ is symmetric for nonsingular $A$\n\nIf $A$ and $B$ are $n\\times n$ symmetric matrices, we have the following properties:\n\n- $A+B$ is symmetric\n\n- The product $A\\cdot B$ is *not* always symmetric. For example, if \n![](images/e4/e41d28b6c6562ada530024fe6ab29cc16447dce14b2f741b8b35758618c78103.svg)\nthen the product $A\\cdot B$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A\\cdot B & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\,\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhich is *not* symmetric.\n\nFinally, the following two properties can be used to construct symmetric matrices:\n\n- $A + A^T$ is symmetric for any *square* matrix $A$\n\n- $A^T A$ and $A A^T$ are symmetric for *any* matrix $A$\n\nThe last property is true for *any* $m\\times n$ matrix. This property is particularly useful, as we'll discover in future lessons." :tutorial/id #uuid "c20572b7-1e75-5be2-a39c-b2327caff234" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5043 :tutorial/title "Properties of Symmetric Matrices"} :step/id #uuid "a119eca9-e695-53d9-9049-eef3212a6e22" :step/math-academy-id 31545 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162925}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162925 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162024 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161816 :question/id #uuid "b159d52a-a436-5743-9151-2e147364a41d" :question/math-academy-id "e-8478"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bcd0ca26-3f22-533f-9d08-1533eeddbc79" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049125} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050446}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162676} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162694} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275135} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275136} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275137} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275138} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360684} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360685} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360686} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365108} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366739} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490230}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Properties of Symmetric Matrices"} :step/id #uuid "dd82fa90-8f6a-5c31-a6bd-faa30ff2ee22" :step/math-academy-id 31546}] :activity/title "Symmetric Matrices" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186162214}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425156} :activity/id #uuid "4147fa8b-5ff8-579d-afae-acd5982a7b2d" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050021 :topic/id #uuid "268943d4-aaa4-5e39-9db5-48d7523acdc3" :topic/math-academy-id 3118} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425155 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360684 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "61f9b779-a82b-5fca-ac7c-8326f052042f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452263} {:answer/id #uuid "e7a19fce-bfec-59e9-b174-01dd728ea53b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452264}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186452263} :answer-field/id #uuid "027ffc36-9391-569a-9d2f-da2a80b366dc" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452979} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c0b1bee2-eead-5899-9436-3a5e41c5d534" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452265} {:answer/id #uuid "5c95db47-f37c-5361-aca9-caa8ca8ccc7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452266}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186452265} :answer-field/id #uuid "3698aa5e-23d2-5ab3-84e9-157fdeec0f61" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452980} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b91e26b4-0173-5667-9919-d76bd3bc5172" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452267} {:answer/id #uuid "9d9e1009-c7de-59b4-8f5d-83345258f1a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452268}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186452268} :answer-field/id #uuid "6cfe9278-620c-5f30-9b43-3e38a539af4d" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452981}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a6c182c5-819c-5206-a031-9309a3557f8d" :question/math-academy-id "q-96589" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** If $A$ is *any*  $n\\times n$ matrix, then ${A}^{2}$ is always symmetric.\n\n- **II.** The product of two $n\\times n$ symmetric matrices is always symmetric.\n\n- **III.** If $A$ is a symmetric matrix, then ${A}^{-1}$ is always symmetric (if it exists)." :question/worked-solution "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. As a counterexample, consider the matrix \n\n$\\,$ $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\nHowever, ${A}^{2}$ is not symmetric:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. As a counterexample, consider the symmetric matrices \n\n$\\,$ $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nHowever, their product is not symmetric:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, for any non-singular symmetric matrix $A,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({A}^{-1})^{T} & =({A}^{T})^{-1} \\\\  & ={A}^{-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "13d711fe-4f91-5fa4-8a59-b8df54fc9061" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425157}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425156 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357611 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5b67bf2d-f434-5cbf-a65f-fe92576ed3fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469638} {:answer/id #uuid "1f304f6a-e85b-5ca9-8056-745663c7ef23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469639}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186469638} :answer-field/id #uuid "04035bc7-0baa-52a2-8819-5785c4397711" :answer-field/key "P" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470431} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a25bb71a-4230-5f5d-aaf6-2df76c7f39c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469640} {:answer/id #uuid "82cc5f65-1d57-56f6-b5fe-cc38b670cf45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469641}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186469641} :answer-field/id #uuid "c9c0a8bf-9374-501d-9297-e531eb25d3f0" :answer-field/key "Q" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470432} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "689daa18-2b9c-5f89-9c4a-2575e6717d8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469642} {:answer/id #uuid "2223f70f-5ec1-579a-a3a0-521b192241b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469643}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186469642} :answer-field/id #uuid "800cf770-f16d-5241-92e2-0bfe2665859c" :answer-field/key "R" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470433}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "225f8bdf-5da4-5137-973e-d6466e5f7f7a" :question/math-academy-id "q-93985" :question/problem "Which of the following matrices are symmetric?\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,Q=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,R=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- $P$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since ${p}_{12}\\ne {p}_{21},$ we obtain $P\\ne {P}^{T}.$\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- $Q$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $Q={Q}^{T}.$\n\n- $R$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since ${r}_{12}\\ne {r}_{21},$ we obtain $R\\ne {R}^{T}.$\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$Q$ only.\""} :step/id #uuid "850fd871-11f1-5a64-9c68-516ffbeed054" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425155}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425157 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425154 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0aa99686-720d-5c83-8792-909de2767703" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "None of the matrices are symmetric" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425148} {:answer/id #uuid "13edd389-e8cd-52c8-8f6e-2c34ed7ad05c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$P$ and $R$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425149} {:answer/id #uuid "33ef6a26-6c4e-56d3-8e19-d9dc460624f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$Q$ and $R$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425150} {:answer/id #uuid "655424c1-b811-5aaf-8538-b1da76023605" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$P$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425151} {:answer/id #uuid "65dc2a15-12f3-5c0d-999e-1bc8c09548b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$Q$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425152}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425150} :answer-field/id #uuid "2dcd0455-6677-5df3-b08a-01a2a95723e9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425153}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ea940e2f-7cf1-5b69-9241-9cde37e5f341" :question/math-academy-id "q-140503" :question/problem "Which of the following matrices are symmetric?\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,Q=[\\begin{aligned}2 & \\sqrt{2} & -2 \\\\ \\sqrt{2} & -1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,R=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- $P$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since ${p}_{12}\\ne {p}_{21},$ we obtain $P\\ne {P}^{T}.$\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- $Q$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $Q={Q}^{T}.$\n\n- $R$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $R={R}^{T}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$Q$ and $R$ only.\""} :step/id #uuid "ae65fa76-f15c-5a90-8603-42143fe53ed6"}] :activity/title "Symmetric Matrices" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425147}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 1080.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134761} :activity/id #uuid "912bbbc1-7dd5-5812-ba95-2d17ed90bd7e" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050264 :topic/id #uuid "3ab583b8-5390-5f48-b28b-1a89db70664f" :topic/math-academy-id 2152} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134761 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135085 :tutorial/content "How do we represent a double integral over a type II region as a repeated integral? \n\nSpecifically, let's consider\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_D (x+2y) \\, \\textrm d A, \n%= \\int_a^b \\int_c^d (x+2y) \\,\\textrm d y\\, \\textrm d x,\n$$\n\nwhere $D$  is the finite region bounded by the lines $x=0,$ $y=0, y=1$ and the curve $x=e^y,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/09/09fa628c26853d48f3775945e856763b5a996c431785ecf4194c879ee881aa78.png)\n\nFirst, notice that $D$ is indeed a type II region. \nTo determine the right and left functions, we draw a horizontal arrow through the region, as usual.\n\n![](images/47/47fffd047fe12aacfbcc98fa34750e7afcb32dd06618f2553c65432828fff328.png)\n\nHence, the region $D$ can be defined as \n\n$$\nD = \\left\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: {\\color{red}0} \\leq y  \\leq {\\color{blue}1}, \\quad {\\color{red}0} \\leq x \\leq {\\color{blue}e^y} \\right\\}.\n$$\n\nNow, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(x+2y)\\,dA & ={\\int }_{(\\text{lower limit})}^{(\\text{upper limit})}[{\\int }_{(\\text{left function})}^{(\\text{right function})}(x+2y)\\text{ }dx]\\text{ }dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{{e}^{y}}(x+2y)\\text{ }dx]\\text{ }dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd that's our answer! Notice that when we have a type II region, \n\n- the limits for $x$ are functions of $y,$ and\n\n- the limits for $y$ are constants." :tutorial/id #uuid "8a9a9898-50b3-5263-9da1-1228ed4ee5e7" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4318 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "212536fd-085f-5bdf-babc-a919d333a568" :step/math-academy-id 26243 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134762}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134762 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133838 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133653 :question/id #uuid "792b0c63-15eb-5b0e-b5d6-01ae5b68eeca" :question/math-academy-id "e-7134"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "40bbc65e-5ee4-5ce6-aaf7-d0f9968c0c51" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050822}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134297} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134299} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268862} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351271} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351272} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351280} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380985} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380992} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382441} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475949} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475963}] :knowledge-point/title "Representing a Double Integral as a Repeated Integral When the Limits are Given"} :step/id #uuid "cde6f31a-9259-5126-ab62-92a7120fde39" :step/math-academy-id 26244 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134763}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134763 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133839 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133654 :question/id #uuid "e005ebb9-6c14-529e-be8f-9b837e45eef1" :question/math-academy-id "e-7135"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2ccbe70d-24f4-5e9c-8536-9ccab99f571b" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134298} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134306} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268867} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268868} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268869} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326857} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351283} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381020} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381027} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382092} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382469} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407920} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483413} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487382}] :knowledge-point/title "Representing a Double Integral as a Repeated Integral When the Limits are Not Given"} :step/id #uuid "38689b31-f1db-5afc-801c-860cb6fc929b" :step/math-academy-id 26245 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134764}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134764 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135086 :tutorial/content "Suppose that we want to evaluate the repeated integral\n\n$$\n\\int_{0}^{1}\\int_{0}^{e^y} (x+2) \\ \\mathrm{d}  x \\, \\mathrm{d}  y.\n$$\n\nNotice that the domain of integration is a type II region. \n\nTo compute this integral, we must bear the following important points in mind.\n\n- We start with the inner integral, as usual.\n\n- When integrating with respect to $x,$ we treat $y$ as though it were a constant.\n\n- The limits of integration for the inner integral are functions of $y.$ Once again, we treat $y$ as though it were a constant and substitute the integration limits as usual. After this step, there should be no more $x$'s in our integral.\n\n- Finally, we compute the outer integral.\n\nLet's apply these steps to solve the given problem.\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{{e}^{y}}(x+2)\\text{ }dx]dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+2x]}_{0}^{{e}^{y}}\\text{ }dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{{e}^{2y}}{2}+2{e}^{y})\\text{ }dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{{e}^{2y}}{2}+2{e}^{y})\\text{ }dy & ={[\\frac{{e}^{2y}}{4}+2{e}^{y}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2}}{4}+2e-(\\frac{{e}^{0}}{4}+2{e}^{0}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}({e}^{2}+8e-9)\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "cf856bdc-a6e0-55d3-a44c-78b2e569f5e0" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4319 :tutorial/title "Evaluating Repeated Integrals With Variable Limits"} :step/id #uuid "6fd8b1b4-a45f-595a-b748-1d95ba70387d" :step/math-academy-id 26246 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134765}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134765 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133840 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133655 :question/id #uuid "ebd5bc16-2df2-5014-a1a2-bdebf2afa195" :question/math-academy-id "e-7136"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fdce40e5-8ffe-52cb-8a8e-ac8eaea982f0" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050207}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134192} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134196} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268863} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323930} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323967} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351287} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351288} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351289} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380999} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381006} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381013} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407921} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475919} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483406} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487361}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Repeated Integral Defined Over a Type II Region"} :step/id #uuid "97348c88-b232-5f44-bd21-15af51c5d116" :step/math-academy-id 26247 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134766}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134766 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133841 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133656 :question/id #uuid "ad2ef01a-d49d-5b17-9066-d5c3970caf31" :question/math-academy-id "e-7137"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "90f7718f-e014-5232-acb1-6773ab79f61d" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134191} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134519} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268871} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268872} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268873} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268875} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268876} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326871} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351305} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381034} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382490} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382594} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407922} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475953} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487395}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Double Integral Defined Over a Type II Region"} :step/id #uuid "0d46d822-c664-59b7-8ccb-0874593592f6" :step/math-academy-id 26248}] :activity/title "Double Integrals Over Type II Regions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186134021}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407923} :activity/id #uuid "dae6af1c-a643-55d8-9eb3-cb620d5edf1b" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050264 :topic/id #uuid "3ab583b8-5390-5f48-b28b-1a89db70664f" :topic/math-academy-id 2152} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407923 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323930 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "022db7d6-74f8-5b70-953b-f7307b30d216" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321167} {:answer/id #uuid "bfcc41a0-cba5-52a7-ad10-f699b98332a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322638} {:answer/id #uuid "e0694915-fef8-5ca3-bf30-944201a25f5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322902} {:answer/id #uuid "f6ee1da1-9032-5800-bb77-448b86b619a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323084} {:answer/id #uuid "f799216a-905a-5238-a6c7-d799f6ea154d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}-\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323092}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323092} :answer-field/id #uuid "1fcad131-34ae-5ed3-80c6-2f5bcfe38d62" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323228}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "aab883d4-1f11-56e3-bc34-cc7b53210399" :question/math-academy-id "q-37685" :question/problem "Calculate ${\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{y}^{1}\\frac{1}{1+{y}^{2}}\\,dx\\,dy.$" :question/worked-solution "The integral is defined over a type II region.\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{y}^{1}\\frac{1}{1+{y}^{2}}\\,dx\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{1+{y}^{2}}[{\\int }_{y}^{1}dx]\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{1+{y}^{2}}[x]_{y}^{1}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{1+{y}^{2}}(1-y)\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{1}{1+{y}^{2}}-\\frac{y}{1+{y}^{2}})\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{1}{1+{y}^{2}}-\\frac{y}{1+{y}^{2}})\\,dy & ={[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡y-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+{y}^{2})}{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡1-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+{1}^{2})}{2}-(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡0-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+{0}^{2})}{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}-\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "04ef475a-c5cf-51ff-ad2a-7ea0479c4f76" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407927}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407924 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407920 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f195090-3d93-59e1-afd9-0a582f1e9403" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{{y}^{2}}^{8-{y}^{2}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)\\,dx]dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407898} {:answer/id #uuid "21e012b2-7e2a-55b3-9d7c-1a341837e391" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{8-{y}^{2}}[{\\int }_{{y}^{2}}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)\\,dx]dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407899} {:answer/id #uuid "2bdf210e-55ed-5de9-80fd-b8bc3c6300fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{8-{y}^{2}}^{{y}^{2}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)\\,dx]dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407900} {:answer/id #uuid "f8af2e6f-7f53-52a5-b469-925f72fac328" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{{y}^{2}}^{8-{y}^{2}}[{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)\\,dx]dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407914} {:answer/id #uuid "f9c41421-3be2-50e8-9e6b-0a900263854c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{2}}[{\\int }_{2}^{8-{y}^{2}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)\\,dx]dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407915}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407898} :answer-field/id #uuid "90293907-fe9e-585f-8155-9888c4d916c1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407917}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "60195b0c-70fb-5843-93ba-a36d5b0f7537" :question/math-academy-id "q-81292" :question/problem "![](images/df/df3acf049579ec4de948d84d04d4fe3012c2232b9699d0471857227f52c78107.png)\n\nIf $D$ is the finite region enclosed by the curves $x={y}^{2},\\,$ $x=8-{y}^{2},$ and $y=0,$ as shown above, then ${∬}_{D}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)\\,dA=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's represent $D$ as a type II region. \n\n- To determine the $y$-limits, we need to find the $y$-coordinates of the points at which the curves intersect. So, we set the following equation and solve it for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8-{y}^{2} & ={y}^{2} \\\\ 8 & =2{y}^{2} \\\\ {y}^{2} & =4 \\\\ y & =\\pm 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $y$-limits are \n$0\\le y\\le 2.$\n\n- To determine the left and right functions, we draw a horizontal arrow from left to right through the region, as usual.\n\n![](images/27/2722224678aeb1f9ea64f42de2dc4fbf6e139350c291de16500957575fd23850.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,{y}^{2}\\le x\\le 8-{y}^{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)\\,dA & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{{y}^{2}}^{8-{y}^{2}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)\\,dx\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{{y}^{2}}^{8-{y}^{2}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)\\,dx]dy\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "1ac49a49-1255-59a8-8c4e-cccd1b1d9a34" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407925}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407925 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407922 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "407ad84e-74ca-5fc0-921f-8337eef03b66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407902} {:answer/id #uuid "9b8add82-176b-5127-abc9-008b7fd4cd65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407907} {:answer/id #uuid "d2256d50-cc9a-51f4-9bdc-9c4c49897e53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407911} {:answer/id #uuid "d506fb6b-f38e-52d5-99c9-4337a51aff70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407912} {:answer/id #uuid "f68c810b-465d-52e2-9a5e-049c583ea1a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407913}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407913} :answer-field/id #uuid "34f449e1-fb1a-5a4b-9b02-dc697805a6ea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407916}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "af73e85d-c908-5a06-97f4-04484193b6c1" :question/math-academy-id "q-81390" :question/problem "![](images/1b/1b03f2ecd03a89d9484e7d28ab4cb34656b288c3a4554c9a4eeacbe9b61f692c.png)\n\nEvaluate the double integral ${∬}_{D}x\\,dA,\\,$\nwhere $D$ is the region enclosed between the curves ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4$ and $y=2-x,$ above the line $y=1,$ as shown in the picture." :question/worked-solution "Notice that $D$ is a type II region. Since $x\\ge 0,$ we obtain the following equations in terms of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4 & \\,⟹\\,x=\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}} \\\\ y=2-x & \\,⟹\\,x=2-y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's determine the limits of integration.\n\n- To determine the $y$-limits, we need to find the $y$-coordinates of the points at which the curves intersect. So, we set the following equation and solve it for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}} & =2-y \\\\ 4-{y}^{2} & =(2-y)^{2} \\\\ 4-{y}^{2} & =4-4y+{y}^{2} \\\\ 2{y}^{2}-4y & =0 \\\\ y(y-2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, since our region lies above the line $y=1,$ the $y$-limits are \n$1\\le y\\le 2.$\n\n- To determine the left and right functions, we draw a horizontal arrow from left to right through the region, as usual.\n\n![](images/f8/f8fedf15e26f64fc344bd7db6ffaa659bd7c130f36f4a39389b6108136836104.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le y\\le 2,\\,2-y\\le x\\le \\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}}\\}.\n$$\n\nSo, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}x\\,dA={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{2-y}^{\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}}}\\,x\\,dx\\,dy\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{2-y}^{\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}}}\\,x\\,dx\\,dy & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}[{\\int }_{2-y}^{\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}}}x\\,dx]dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\,\\frac{1}{2}[\\,{x}^{2}\\,]_{2-y}^{\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}}}\\text{ }dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\,\\frac{1}{2}(4-{y}^{2}-(2-y)^{2})dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\,(2y-{y}^{2})dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\,(2y-{y}^{2})dy & ={[{y}^{2}-\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =(4-\\frac{8}{3})-(1-\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "6b60b51b-f512-550b-bd86-63bfa17217f5"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407926 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134519 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6e8f08bf-1207-5b03-b3ef-9530cb2b5a3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "e+\\frac{1}{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407903} {:answer/id #uuid "84cf6de9-7e2a-5324-b99e-6bcb99cb2037" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2e-\\frac{2}{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407904} {:answer/id #uuid "961f6414-5e96-5d6f-8c75-fea444892c6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "e+\\frac{1}{e}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407906} {:answer/id #uuid "c4e79523-6046-58bb-bbff-c9138272412a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{2}+\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407909} {:answer/id #uuid "c58ab0f6-8f1f-5fab-87ce-c1f3defe9063" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "e-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407910}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407906} :answer-field/id #uuid "c010f524-8a53-5bfa-911a-2af5f0f0f3d6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407918}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "93a9e190-c192-5836-a0b9-91bcfd91a0ef" :question/math-academy-id "q-81197" :question/problem "Calculate ${∬}_{D}{e}^{x-y}\\,dA,$ where $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,0\\le x\\le 2y}.$" :question/worked-solution "The integral is defined over a type II region $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,0\\le x\\le 2y}.$\n\nSo, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}{e}^{x-y}\\,dA & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2y}({e}^{x}\\cdot {e}^{-y})\\,dx\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2y}({e}^{x}\\cdot {e}^{-y})\\,dx\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{-y}[{\\int }_{0}^{2y}{e}^{x}\\,dx]dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{-y}[{e}^{x}]_{0}^{2y}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{-y}({e}^{2y}-{e}^{0})dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({e}^{y}-{e}^{-y})\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({e}^{y}-{e}^{-y})\\,dy & =[{e}^{y}+{e}^{-y}]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =({e}^{1}+{e}^{-1})-({e}^{0}+{e}^{0}) \\\\  & =e+\\frac{1}{e}-2\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "ac90c75a-a52d-53a2-be62-82bea3febc78" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407924}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407927 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407921 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a312eff-692f-5f36-add7-8d6f7a0791cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407896} {:answer/id #uuid "1deb603f-c9fd-595d-8f3d-42a462139aa4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1)+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407897} {:answer/id #uuid "32f8a409-127f-58c3-b517-447a5ac20acd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407901} {:answer/id #uuid "8b2724c9-647b-522d-8e19-f98d5782dae8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1)-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407905} {:answer/id #uuid "b459c99e-957e-52e0-9bb4-92fd9e78c9d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407908}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407901} :answer-field/id #uuid "ef9310b5-68fe-5622-806f-a4683ba15fec" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407919}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "6b2d2a45-1af2-5bb8-b665-008434b3075e" :question/math-academy-id "q-37855" :question/problem "${\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{y}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,dx\\,dy=$" :question/worked-solution "The integral is defined over a type II region.\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{y}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,dx\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{y}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,dx]\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡x]_{0}^{y}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-\\operatorname{sin}⁡0)\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,dy & =[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1)-(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1)\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "e5e63a66-bea9-51c1-84d4-5364df4be361" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407926}}] :activity/title "Double Integrals Over Type II Regions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407895}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483490} :activity/id #uuid "deb6a015-5188-5c6d-9bbf-340e569612da" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050264 :topic/id #uuid "3ab583b8-5390-5f48-b28b-1a89db70664f" :topic/math-academy-id 2152} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483448 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483406 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "446d2ae8-dc82-50e7-896b-5418a7d5cfcc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482930} {:answer/id #uuid "bb439f1a-410c-5eb8-8f44-0f5d3790b885" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{30}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483127} {:answer/id #uuid "beec0d21-4472-5b7e-8a67-4ff29eca2542" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483132} {:answer/id #uuid "d948eeb8-aa2f-59a3-84a4-d8a991007f59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483178} {:answer/id #uuid "ef86a85c-17ca-562f-9487-9b3559e88804" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483213}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483127} :answer-field/id #uuid "899b1e4d-10a7-5a27-99db-9badc378d5e8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483293}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "c2f72ec4-b491-5de0-a559-269634816f5a" :question/math-academy-id "q-81312" :question/problem "${\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{y}^{3}}^{{y}^{2}}(y-1)\\,dx\\,dy=$" :question/worked-solution "The integral is defined over a type II region.\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{y}^{3}}^{{y}^{2}}(y-1)\\,dx\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(y-1)[{\\int }_{{y}^{3}}^{{y}^{2}}\\,dx]\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(y-1)\\,[x]_{{y}^{3}}^{{y}^{2}}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(y-1)({y}^{2}-{y}^{3})\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(-{y}^{4}+2{y}^{3}-{y}^{2})\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(-{y}^{4}+2{y}^{3}-{y}^{2})\\,dy & ={[-\\frac{{y}^{5}}{5}+\\frac{{y}^{4}}{2}-\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}+\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{30}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "2afa0257-b116-5a6a-9b0d-60d7a1cd57fb" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483494}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483482 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483413 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0dbf9137-cd18-5b00-8628-35682148d59b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{{y}^{3}}^{3-{y}^{3}}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[3]{3/2}}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{xy}}\\,dx]dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482833} {:answer/id #uuid "27f8b3fc-0f1b-53d7-9969-248547e7b45b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{3-{y}^{3}}[{\\int }_{{y}^{3}}^{\\sqrt[3]{3/2}}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{xy}}\\,dx]dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482883} {:answer/id #uuid "5807c016-a68d-500a-bafd-bbe8e52e28c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{{y}^{3}}^{\\sqrt[3]{3/2}}[{\\int }_{0}^{3-{y}^{3}}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{xy}}\\,dx]dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482967} {:answer/id #uuid "7d7b1686-130b-5256-a8cd-ed668ee1fd00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[3]{3/2}}[{\\int }_{{y}^{3}}^{3-{y}^{3}}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{xy}}\\,dx]dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483032} {:answer/id #uuid "e13baa75-b4d0-5922-8e2d-cba1a3bda445" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[3]{3/2}}[{\\int }_{3-{y}^{3}}^{{y}^{3}}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{xy}}\\,dx]dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483193}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483032} :answer-field/id #uuid "d7bebbb6-2b2e-5637-b8dd-0175f7869ed4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483324}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "cf43c215-ffc3-5823-9335-496b6c1fd83f" :question/math-academy-id "q-81301" :question/problem "![](images/c3/c3dbd6e26cb67b2f247387c30e03296f790c3f56c03779a7dda5ae5d0b081958.png)\n\nIf $D$ is the finite region enclosed by the lines $x=3-{y}^{3},\\,$ $x={y}^{3},$ and $y=0,$ as shown above, then ${∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{xy}}\\,dA=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's represent $D$ as a type II region. \n\n- To determine the $y$-limits, we need to find the $y$-coordinates of the points at which the curves intersect. So, we set the following equation and solve it for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3-{y}^{3} & ={y}^{3} \\\\ 2{y}^{3} & =3 \\\\ {y}^{3} & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ y & =\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{3}{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $y$-limits are \n$0\\le y\\le \\sqrt[3]{\\frac{3}{2}}.$\n\n- To determine the left and right functions, we draw a horizontal arrow from left to right through the region, as usual.\n\n![](images/36/366146b42d476b8d2a467e607a3bba1ef65ddc8a344b3f0e17ee4652ccc0463a.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le \\sqrt[3]{\\frac{3}{2}},\\,{y}^{3}\\le x\\le 3-{y}^{3}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{xy}}\\,dA & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[3]{3/2}}{\\int }_{{y}^{3}}^{3-{y}^{3}}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{xy}}\\,dx\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[3]{3/2}}[{\\int }_{{y}^{3}}^{3-{y}^{3}}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{xy}}\\,dx]dy\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "9ace463d-182e-59e5-aeba-5e1e35ffd968" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483448}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483490 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134306 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28685095-749a-5991-9be2-402bf87a4783" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{2}^{y/2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{y}}f(x,y)\\,dx\\,dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475495} {:answer/id #uuid "b50cbc81-f2c6-5823-bd95-abc058fa081b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{2}^{\\sqrt{y}}{\\int }_{0}^{y/2}f(x,y)\\,dx\\,dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475713} {:answer/id #uuid "c020b011-abec-5f74-a0d6-2e493cdf3a8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{y}}{\\int }_{2}^{y/2}f(x,y)\\,dx\\,dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475735} {:answer/id #uuid "ce6d84dc-f6bf-5a95-aef6-2d114a0e442f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{2}^{4}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{y}}^{y/2}f(x,y)\\,dx\\,dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475751} {:answer/id #uuid "dc4f557a-0393-5b7c-9aa1-df8febbf44fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{2}^{4}{\\int }_{y/2}^{\\sqrt{y}}f(x,y)\\,dx\\,dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475776}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475776} :answer-field/id #uuid "2cda2ff4-5dd7-546b-a1e2-111a796413b5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131875}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "118a9fcd-c8c0-57b4-8e3a-7c92ff45ac8e" :question/math-academy-id "q-40678" :question/problem "Consider the finite region $D$ in the $xy$-plane enclosed between the curves $y=2x$ and $y={x}^{2}$ above the line $y=2.$ What is ${∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA\\,$ expressed as a repeated integral?" :question/worked-solution "First, let's make a sketch of our region.\n\n![](images/4e/4e58d7969f0b31206ae8c3385a857c0ad6cf8a42ee79909d51c3a6b58a090852.png)\n\nWe need to represent $D$ as a type II region. Writing $y=2x$ and $y={x}^{2}$ in terms of $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=2x & \\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{y}{2} \\\\ y={x}^{2} & \\,⟹\\,x=\\sqrt{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's determine the limits of integration.\n\n- To determine the $y$-limits, we need to find the $y$-coordinates of the points at which the curves intersect. So, we set the following equation and solve it for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{y} & =\\frac{y}{2} \\\\ y & =\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} \\\\ {y}^{2}-4y & =0 \\\\ y(y-4) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, since our region lies above the line $y=2,$ the $y$-limits are \n$2\\le y\\le 4.$\n\n- To determine the left and right functions, we draw a horizontal arrow from left to right through the region, as usual.\n\n![](images/16/16944d6651297f1c13c39ddf3d9160d721f5f29bfa6b0acac0db46c88295964e.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,2\\le y\\le 4,\\,\\frac{y}{2}\\le x\\le \\sqrt{y}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA & ={\\int }_{2}^{4}{\\int }_{y/2}^{\\sqrt{y}}f(x,y)\\,dx\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "b77fee23-8b22-50d7-96e8-1c58b86a3b31" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483482}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483494 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351288 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "054cfdb8-9523-5fd3-8ee5-f3584ca875a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348495} {:answer/id #uuid "1a58f340-5f07-524b-9972-7aec7a072fe6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348668} {:answer/id #uuid "413f25e1-e820-5b99-b415-72140d39e76a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349040} {:answer/id #uuid "630cd449-0bac-53d3-aade-e06771d4eb3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349353} {:answer/id #uuid "df055118-5d65-5cb6-afdd-037e1bc8f8e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350398}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348668} :answer-field/id #uuid "bb3e7c8f-9e68-503e-917d-bfcf6dae85d1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351030}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f40afbe3-1000-5dc9-b06f-6a8edbf20249" :question/math-academy-id "q-81315" :question/problem "Calculate ${\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3\\pi /2}}{\\int }_{0}^{y}\\operatorname{cos}⁡{y}^{2}\\,dx\\,dy.$" :question/worked-solution "The integral is defined over a type II region.\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3\\pi /2}}{\\int }_{0}^{y}\\operatorname{cos}⁡{y}^{2}\\,dx\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3\\pi /2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡{y}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{y}dx]dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3\\pi /2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡{y}^{2}[x]_{0}^{y}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3\\pi /2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡{y}^{2}(y-0)\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3\\pi /2}}y\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡{y}^{2}\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3\\pi /2}}y\\operatorname{cos}⁡{y}^{2}\\,dy & ={[\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{\\sqrt{3\\pi /2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(-1-0) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "c80c8b7b-b893-5376-b09c-6e111f90842a" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483497}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483497 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268871 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2268decf-8057-5052-b22c-2d9db7931297" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266451} {:answer/id #uuid "2b64e25b-869e-5a23-a2e6-e9bcb899719b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266528} {:answer/id #uuid "477faab4-1a81-5e30-989d-335ba513d783" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266784} {:answer/id #uuid "a3a5dd31-4b38-5d68-841b-b0fe947ea52a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267616} {:answer/id #uuid "f1267f21-e92c-5711-a63a-3abce564f530" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268288}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266784} :answer-field/id #uuid "c4dfa161-4dd2-52cf-bd87-7379b870a0f3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268757}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "5c4cd592-9607-50f1-89a9-e38280b49bc5" :question/math-academy-id "q-132527" :question/problem "Calculate ${∬}_{D}y\\,dA,$ where $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,2{y}^{2}\\le x\\le {y}^{2}+1\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "The integral is defined over a type II region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,2{y}^{2}\\le x\\le {y}^{2}+1\\}.$\n\nSo, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}y\\,dA & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{2{y}^{2}}^{{y}^{2}+1}y\\,dx\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{2{y}^{2}}^{{y}^{2}+1}y\\,dx\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}y[{\\int }_{2{y}^{2}}^{{y}^{2}+1}\\,dx]dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}y[\\,x\\,]_{2{y}^{2}}^{{y}^{2}+1}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}y({y}^{2}+1-2{y}^{2})dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(y-{y}^{3})\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(y-{y}^{3})\\,dy & ={[\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}{y}^{4}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "d0735f10-459e-5cba-a81d-a0fc2a9e4e82"}] :activity/title "Double Integrals Over Type II Regions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482811}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487443} :activity/id #uuid "81417b33-9cfb-5279-ac5e-fa21eea69292" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050264 :topic/id #uuid "3ab583b8-5390-5f48-b28b-1a89db70664f" :topic/math-academy-id 2152} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487407 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487361 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23fc295d-ebc5-5a8b-8333-139d985dd2cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486666} {:answer/id #uuid "46e4ae08-be52-5583-8476-6a01197ebd93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486747} {:answer/id #uuid "ad96f4d5-21d0-5dbb-8b99-aac52f757cc0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486982} {:answer/id #uuid "edca23a3-48cf-59c9-8421-e5dd35d40324" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487099} {:answer/id #uuid "f4de4982-fe7b-5fdf-9e7a-721865ec64c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487119}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486666} :answer-field/id #uuid "dff5d093-dd4c-5ad2-a908-7ac3e6eb6096" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487262}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a3c08bbb-13dd-5937-9a36-d4dc386d9f8f" :question/math-academy-id "q-132418" :question/problem "Calculate ${\\int }_{-2}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}}}3x\\,dx\\,dy.$" :question/worked-solution "The integral is defined over a type II region.\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-2}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}}}3x\\,dx\\,dy & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}}}3x\\,dx]dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}{[\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}]}_{0}^{\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}}}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}\\frac{3}{2}(4-{y}^{2})\\,dy \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{-2}^{2}(4-{y}^{2})\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{-2}^{2}(4-{y}^{2})\\,dy & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot 2{\\int }_{0}^{2}(4-{y}^{2})\\,dy \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{2}(4-{y}^{2})\\,dy \\\\  & =3{[4y-\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{3}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =3(8-\\frac{8}{3}) \\\\  & =3(\\frac{16}{3}) \\\\  & =16\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "02dcebbb-e0ab-58c9-9244-58670d1f8782" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487420}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487420 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487382 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0afd5248-63a6-54b0-82c9-25e9a06bc6a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{6-y}^{\\sqrt{y}}f(x,y)\\,dx\\,dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486616} {:answer/id #uuid "2f40ccc9-c08d-5c80-8bf5-64675b6b4f8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{y}}^{6-y}f(x,y)\\,dx\\,dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486688} {:answer/id #uuid "4c531ca2-1a5c-512b-95e2-ef39648f7a65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\sqrt{y}}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{6-y}f(x,y)\\,dx\\,dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486758} {:answer/id #uuid "9ebc02a7-5c9e-5d8e-89aa-6707f0d4e2b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\sqrt{y}}^{6-y}{\\int }_{0}^{4}f(x,y)\\,dx\\,dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486951} {:answer/id #uuid "fe72c14f-3623-5add-8ee7-086a6b32d59e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{6-y}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{y}}^{4}f(x,y)\\,dx\\,dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487135}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486688} :answer-field/id #uuid "fcdeecd0-b7a7-5385-bca4-86e41c8c6926" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487284}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "cbaf5fee-0009-5eb1-a042-591a8b53019b" :question/math-academy-id "q-132514" :question/problem "Consider the finite region $D$ in the first quadrant enclosed by the $x$-axis and the curves $y={x}^{2}$ and $y=6-x.$ What is ${∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA\\,$ expressed as a repeated integral?" :question/worked-solution "First, let's make a sketch of our region.\n\n![](images/8c/8c55ebba1610708143086512788e64464e8b50a517257bd63a1c28b89ee7327d.png)\n\nWe need to represent $D$ as a type II region. Writing $y={x}^{2}$ and $y=6-x$ in terms of $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y={x}^{2} & \\,⟹\\,x=\\sqrt{y} \\\\ y=6-x & \\,⟹\\,x=6-y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's determine the limits of integration.\n\n- To determine the $y$-limits, we need to find the $y$-coordinates of the points at which the curves intersect. So, we set the following equation and solve it for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{y} & =6-y \\\\ (\\sqrt{y})^{2} & =(6-y)^{2} \\\\ y & =36-12y+{y}^{2} \\\\ {y}^{2}-13y+36 & =0 \\\\ (y-4)(y-9) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe discard $y=9$ since it does not satisfy the first equality. Therefore, the $y$-limits are \n$0\\le y\\le 4.$\n\n- To determine the left and right functions, we draw a horizontal arrow from left to right through the region, as usual.\n\n![](images/c9/c9c3e3bdec1a007c47d8d1251b790b970a0d367304a5b1e5244bec014e69580d.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 4,\\,\\sqrt{y}\\le x\\le 6-y\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{y}}^{6-y}f(x,y)\\,dx\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "369ae1cd-6bd8-5c9c-993d-8f69974de934" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487451}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487431 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487395 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12d4b5f4-4597-5fb2-8661-87271390504e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486634} {:answer/id #uuid "a4f2da0d-8ced-511f-9af3-03d20168e670" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1)}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486968} {:answer/id #uuid "af82d238-ad19-5d0c-84ab-db2ccd88c886" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486987} {:answer/id #uuid "f733835b-7fab-5fb8-8f67-caf01a296a96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1)}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487122} {:answer/id #uuid "faee4b2e-60b6-5fd3-8b48-4fdb4f844b38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1)-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487132}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487122} :answer-field/id #uuid "eaed71bb-0e77-5fb5-88f5-a08c5de3d01d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487271}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "e726e2b9-ad90-5a69-bca6-00fc64c93333" :question/math-academy-id "q-89549" :question/problem "Calculate ${∬}_{D}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡{y}^{3}}{{y}^{2}}\\,dA,$\nwhere $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le y\\le \\sqrt[3]{\\frac{\\pi }{2}},\\,0\\le x\\le {y}^{4}\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "The integral is defined over a type II region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le y\\le \\sqrt[3]{\\frac{\\pi }{2}},\\,0\\le x\\le {y}^{4}\\}.$\n\nSo, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡{y}^{3}}{{y}^{2}}\\,dA & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt[3]{\\pi /2}}{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{4}}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡{y}^{3}}{{y}^{2}}\\,dx\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt[3]{\\pi /2}}{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{4}}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡{y}^{3}}{{y}^{2}}\\,dx\\,dy & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt[3]{\\pi /2}}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡{y}^{3}}{{y}^{2}}[{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{4}}dx]\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt[3]{\\pi /2}}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡{y}^{3}}{{y}^{2}}[x]_{0}^{{y}^{4}}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt[3]{\\pi /2}}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡{y}^{3}}{{y}^{2}}({y}^{4}-0)\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt[3]{\\pi /2}}{y}^{2}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡{y}^{3}\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt[3]{\\pi /2}}{y}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡{y}^{3}\\,dy & ={[-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡{y}^{3}}{3}]}_{1}^{\\sqrt[3]{\\pi /2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1)}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "668cc3f4-24bc-52b7-a6ae-eb045c8d90e0" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487407}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487443 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134298 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5bc9fc65-1739-5a7c-8405-faa5b206275d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{4/3}[{\\int }_{y/2}^{2-y}xy\\,dx]dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324729} {:answer/id #uuid "68b38b5d-aad2-529b-a2d8-79a2b6ddb413" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{4/3}[{\\int }_{2-y}^{y/2}xy\\,dx]dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324845} {:answer/id #uuid "a7d2cf74-7d71-5a5b-ac1a-00940077dba2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{y/2}[{\\int }_{0}^{2-y}xy\\,dx]dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325399} {:answer/id #uuid "ae4c1494-e680-57a5-bec4-f41594ee3b5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{2-y}^{y/2}[{\\int }_{0}^{4/3}xy\\,dx]dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325450} {:answer/id #uuid "f44c68bb-fac5-5af3-ac88-abac96d890b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{y/2}^{2-y}[{\\int }_{0}^{4/3}xy\\,dx]dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326121}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186324729} :answer-field/id #uuid "7d4129ae-aa8f-5600-8743-4d0691281050" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326448}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "89052f85-16e5-5038-8862-a854c23cc74d" :question/math-academy-id "q-40596" :question/problem "![](images/c7/c752eb32fc94ae630ef3998c81a6b9b0af5c222a367f87db8bbacb877716c16c.png)\n\nIf $D$ is the finite region enclosed by the lines $x=2-y,\\,$ $x=\\frac{y}{2},$ and $y=0,$ as shown above, then ${∬}_{D}xy\\,dA=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's represent $D$ as a type II region. \n\n- To determine the $y$-limits, we need to find the $y$-coordinates of the points at which the curves intersect. So, we set the following equation and solve it for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2-y & =\\frac{y}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2}y & =-2 \\\\ y & =\\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $y$-limits are \n$0\\le y\\le \\frac{4}{3}.$\n\n- To determine the left and right functions, we draw a horizontal arrow from left to right through the region, as usual.\n\n![](images/e6/e6c9332f8f59e2561af9ba5aa89311263fb772dc987ed0b15d91a6d5eea5cf8e.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le \\frac{4}{3},\\,\\frac{y}{2}\\le x\\le 2-y\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}xy\\,dA & ={\\int }_{0}^{4/3}{\\int }_{y/2}^{2-y}xy\\,dx\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4/3}[{\\int }_{y/2}^{2-y}xy\\,dx]dy\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "d5945b0d-9036-5506-98f2-814b1e016d1b" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487431}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487451 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268872 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b299a6b-4484-532a-9f8c-c14f516cc562" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{8}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266245} {:answer/id #uuid "276d6c64-ff31-5537-9cfc-e67b94462ffd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{8}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266496} {:answer/id #uuid "625522d9-ae80-5048-aff1-7d51ffb91b4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{4}+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267009} {:answer/id #uuid "683122b3-f461-5f8c-9000-a8fdb77f528a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{4}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267067} {:answer/id #uuid "77f6ea70-051f-52ad-bfa3-1aafd5a5a61d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{8}+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267195}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266245} :answer-field/id #uuid "5098efbb-c619-55aa-9d7a-009fdc05844c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268550}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c2534dab-da9e-52d9-8510-a8b1311ad552" :question/math-academy-id "q-132529" :question/problem "Calculate ${∬}_{D}x{e}^{{y}^{3}}\\,dA,$ where $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,-y\\le x\\le 2y\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "The integral is defined over a type II region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,-y\\le x\\le 2y\\}.$\n\nSo, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}x{e}^{{y}^{3}}\\,dA & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{-y}^{2y}x{e}^{{y}^{3}}\\,dx\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{-y}^{2y}x{e}^{{y}^{3}}\\,dx\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{{y}^{3}}[{\\int }_{-y}^{2y}x\\,dx]dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{{y}^{3}}{[\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}]}_{-y}^{2y}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{{y}^{3}}(2{y}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2})dy \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}3{y}^{2}{e}^{{y}^{3}}\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}3{y}^{2}{e}^{{y}^{3}}\\,dy & =\\frac{1}{2}{[{e}^{{y}^{3}}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{8}-{e}^{0}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{8}-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "ef76088b-8705-5bf1-a9f2-8f6662b74350"}] :activity/title "Double Integrals Over Type II Regions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486590}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 600.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163156} :activity/id #uuid "4373edc4-1d40-5e9a-bfe9-ecc0845c2651" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050835 :topic/id #uuid "6a5936fb-0bab-5b84-a342-e13ec8019ebc" :topic/math-academy-id 3048} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163156 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163365 :tutorial/content "Let $X$ and $Y$ be discrete random variables with supports $S_X$ and $S_Y,$ respectively. We say that $X$ and $Y$ are **independent** if, for all $x\\in S_X$ and $y\\in S_Y,$ we have \n\n$$\nP(X=x, Y=y)=P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y).\n$$\n\nMoreover, if $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x,y),$ and $f_X(x)$ and $f_Y(y)$ are the marginal mass functions of $X$ and $Y,$ respectively, then\n\n$$\nf(x, y) = f_X(x)\\cdot f_Y (y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all $x\\in S_X$ and $y\\in S_Y.$\n\nIf the random variables are not independent, they are **dependent**." :tutorial/id #uuid "ab3a2705-ec4d-5e72-b6dd-553e9cbb6884" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7746 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "b566ab50-5b2d-5693-aaa2-b74aeb985d53" :step/math-academy-id 44847 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163159}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163157 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162153 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161713 :question/id #uuid "1fdcc887-4c52-5d91-ab28-63a7dce9fe5a" :question/math-academy-id "e-12462"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "196e7d31-5af8-5c82-b2bd-0cf1ed7415e2" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162343} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162388} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275114} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275115} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275116} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275117} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275118} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383466} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383473} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426030}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Two Random Variables are Independent Given the Joint PMF Only"} :step/id #uuid "85e8d07f-ff5e-5a08-b15e-b9682157622a" :step/math-academy-id 44848 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163158}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163158 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162154 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161714 :question/id #uuid "4e12d6b6-5771-5e54-8f16-7899f82d90bc" :question/math-academy-id "e-12463"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "900dcaf8-da39-5654-96cd-6aec89e379c9" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162304} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162305} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275113} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275221} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281557} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281558} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383459} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383582} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410436} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426031} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426032}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Joint Probabilities"} :step/id #uuid "3bf9c7bd-4281-5c38-aa27-271d95010465" :step/math-academy-id 44851} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163159 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162155 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161715 :question/id #uuid "1875d098-95f6-50a7-b069-56865207b3dc" :question/math-academy-id "e-12464"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "35de7257-8d35-5f81-89be-c7faa73d6bc3" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048325} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048945}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162457} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162458} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281533} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281553} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281683} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284383} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481664}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Two Random Variables are Independent"} :step/id #uuid "b0b7073a-7020-52ef-b9e8-f169d03cd0f4" :step/math-academy-id 44854 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163157}}] :activity/title "Independence of Discrete Random Variables" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186162186}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 660.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120884} :activity/id #uuid "dcdc8ecc-c5d8-56ba-b664-0870beb832b2" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051046 :topic/id #uuid "7cf616eb-2b54-52d2-8407-1b2fc912b2d9" :topic/math-academy-id 1853} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120884 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121126 :tutorial/content "A **subspace** of $\\mathbb{R}^n$ is a subset of $\\mathbb R^n$ that is closed  under vector addition and scalar multiplication. In other words: \n\n$H\\subseteq \\mathbb R^n$ is a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^n$ if and only if it satisfies the following properties:\n\n- *Closure under vector addition:* \n\n$\\qquad$ If $\\mathbf{u}$ and $\\mathbf{v}$ are in $H,$ then their sum $\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v}$ is also in $H.$\n\n- *Closure under scalar multiplication:* \n\n$\\qquad$ If $\\mathbf{u}$ is in $H,$ then $c\\cdot \\mathbf{u}$ is also in $H$ for any scalar number $c.$\n\nFor example, consider the line $L$ with equation $y=3x$ in $\\mathbb{R}^2.$ Is $L$ a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^2?$ \n\nFirst, notice that we can express the points on $L$ as the following set:\n\n![](images/1f/1f380f9928da2dd44ff157a72fce97c02ae44626df515d52ece6ff5feee302d2.svg)\n\nIn other words, $L$ contains all vectors of the form ![](images/90/90996c2ffae29c3bd9cd3ab398cca4dd7e1b8fbe275de8520a72a16e504fb0f3.svg) where $x$ is a real number.\n\nTo determine whether $L$ is a subspace of $\\mathbb R^2,$ we need to check the two conditions above. \n\n- Let's pick two arbitrary vectors in $L,$ namely, ![](images/59/59be22e80d0be32c84a8c2bdb2f2c96b23a0941d4aba26bdefa078fd299c137c.svg) and ![](images/d7/d78ea4fcfbd469e88b7fe46e28ac6f396a538c577f4defdd1b3d60e60df3c4b7.svg) If we compute their sum, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u+v & =[\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} \\\\ 3{u}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1} \\\\ 3{v}_{1}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}+{v}_{1} \\\\ 3{u}_{1}+3{v}_{1}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}+{v}_{1} \\\\ 3\\cdot ({u}_{1}+{v}_{1})\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the condition $y=3x.$ Therefore, $\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v} \\in L. \\quad {\\color{green}\\checkmark}$\n\n- Let's pick an arbitrary vector in $L,$ namely, ![](images/75/759c7883ad2772f20a090ad2f8c5dfe01031ca31e2b5f0e7681c945c9256c91d.svg) Then, for any scalar $c$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c\\cdot u & =c\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} \\\\ 3{u}_{1}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}c{u}_{1} \\\\ 3\\cdot c{u}_{1}\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the condition $y=3x.$ Therefore, $c\\cdot \\mathbf{u}\\in L. \\quad {\\color{green}\\checkmark}$\n\nThe set $L$ satisfies both conditions. So, we can conclude that it is a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^2.$\n\n**Note:** Since the second condition must be satisfied for any $c,$ including $c=0,$ we can conclude that every subspace must contain the zero vector $\\mathbf{0}.$ So, if a set does not contain $\\mathbf{0}$ then it is *not* a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^n.$\n\nFor instance, the set \n\n![](images/a1/a15e0b4fc4544421e572d15b8fd016c3fa8721e0ecf3f8808a469c3252d1f6d6.svg)\n\ndoes *not* contain the zero vector (since $0 \\neq 0 + 1$), and consequently it is *not* a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^2.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "ee2c580c-5386-5e06-9b58-e9e1dd398cd3" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6853 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "7f009ee8-4a5e-5da7-b539-b2991cbd9d04" :step/math-academy-id 41645 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120885}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120885 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119926 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119500 :question/id #uuid "d2cb7f5f-ad04-5e04-b2da-0f9b555442a3" :question/math-academy-id "e-10974"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "19c33c51-bf11-5a81-a0fd-8381d8e0b9d7" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048499} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049716} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050367}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120145} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120150} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275357} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275358} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275359} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275361} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275389} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417219}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Line Is a Subspace of Two-Dimensional Space"} :step/id #uuid "a7eb434d-3210-5f2f-b5ec-18781d0fa02d" :step/math-academy-id 41646 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120888}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120886 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119927 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119501 :question/id #uuid "d0d76105-c49e-5cf8-af57-bd74989e895b" :question/math-academy-id "e-10977"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "83796fe8-f045-5dbc-94d1-6d6e031f3ed8" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048850} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049485}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120146} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120151} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275363} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275390} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275391} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275392} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275393} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275394} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186376054} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425728} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489255}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Line Is a Subspace of Three-Dimensional Space"} :step/id #uuid "911d42c3-5fd0-515e-abcd-cfaab6aa6926" :step/math-academy-id 41660 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120889}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120887 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119928 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119502 :question/id #uuid "d2199de1-4bfe-5589-95dd-f9bb7c339437" :question/math-academy-id "e-10980"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f3018fd-9d16-578f-b050-966890844c9b" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050830}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120152} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120153} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275364} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275395} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275396} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275397} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275398} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275399} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186376047} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186376061} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186376068} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489173}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Plane Is a Subspace of Three-Dimensional Space"} :step/id #uuid "8b4b4898-6c15-52d9-96fe-d57251b08369" :step/math-academy-id 41667} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120888 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121127 :tutorial/content "Recall that $H$ is a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^n$ if and only if it satisfies the following properties:\n\n*Closure under vector addition:* \n\n $\\qquad$ If $\\mathbf{u}$ and $\\mathbf{v}$ are in $H,$ then their sum $\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v}$ is also in $H.$\n\n*Closure under scalar multiplication:* \n\n $\\qquad$ If $\\mathbf{u}$ is in $H,$ then $c\\cdot \\mathbf{u}$ is also in $H$ for any scalar number $c.$\n\nLet $L$ be the line in $\\mathbb{R}^3$ that passes through the points $(0,0,0)$ and $(4,1,-2).$ Is $L$ a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^3?$ \n\nFirst, note that the direction vector of the line $L$ is \n![](images/25/25fa34994b33e0d11fd8990dddbfda276857e699018b4701c6e2eaf13acb0c20.svg)\nThis means that the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4t \\\\ t \\\\ -2t\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor $t\\in\\mathbb R,$ and the corresponding Cartesian equation is \n\n$$\n\\dfrac x4=y=\\dfrac z{-2}.\n$$\n\nTo determine whether $L$ is a subspace, we need to check the two conditions above:\n\n- Let's pick two arbitrary vectors in $L,$ namely, ![](images/d7/d7f1a3cf1625ff12bc96324dc428e1c95d271d8a72de603b671f722b9173281d.svg) and ![](images/25/255fc61225b017a51ae60b1d157787ac4b60fe596ebaa8296b48053b87b2eae7.svg) If we compute their sum, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u+v & =[\\begin{aligned}4{u}_{1} \\\\ {u}_{1} \\\\ -2{u}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}4{v}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{1} \\\\ -2{v}_{1}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4{u}_{1}+4{v}_{1} \\\\ {u}_{1}+{v}_{1} \\\\ (-2{u}_{1})+(-2{v}_{1})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4\\cdot ({u}_{1}+{v}_{1}) \\\\ {u}_{1}+{v}_{1} \\\\ (-2)\\cdot ({u}_{1}+{v}_{1})\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the condition $\\dfrac x4=y=\\dfrac z{-2}.$ Therefore, $\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v}\\in L. \\quad {\\color{green}\\checkmark}$\n\n- Let's pick an arbitrary vector in $L,$ namely, ![](images/7f/7f7df4272eae1975b5802d54dfae499c528661ba0ae82faff45bb26f6cfab276.svg) Then, for any scalar $c,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c\\cdot u & =c\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4{u}_{1} \\\\ {u}_{1} \\\\ -2{u}_{1}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4\\cdot c{u}_{1} \\\\ c{u}_{1} \\\\ (-2)\\cdot c{u}_{1}\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the condition $\\dfrac x4=y=\\dfrac z{-2}.$ Therefore, $c\\cdot \\mathbf{u}\\in L. \\quad {\\color{green}\\checkmark}$\n\nThe set $L$ satisfies both conditions. So, we can conclude that it is a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^3.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "fb0bb214-7f56-557a-82f7-baeef815457e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6859 :tutorial/title "Lines as Subspaces of Three-Dimensional Space"} :step/id #uuid "124c571f-28f0-56fa-a68d-76ef241f4618" :step/math-academy-id 41674 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120886}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120889 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121128 :tutorial/content "Again, recall that $H$ is a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^n$ if and only if it satisfies the following properties:\n\n- *Closure under vector addition:* \n\n$\\qquad$ If $\\mathbf{u}$ and $\\mathbf{v}$ are in $H,$ then their sum $\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v}$ is also in $H.$\n\n- *Closure under scalar multiplication:* \n\n$\\qquad$ If $\\mathbf{u}$ is in $H,$ then $c\\cdot \\mathbf{u}$ is also in $H$ for any scalar number $c.$\n\nConsider the plane $\\Pi$ with equation $z=4x-2y$ in $\\mathbb{R}^3.$ \n\nNotice that $\\Pi$ contains vectors of the form \n\n![](images/95/956757aa0ce5b4721db582cd9d03dffaa21b1fdf6f8c60b5c6465f9cf012201d.svg)\n\nIs $\\Pi$ a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^3?$ To find out, we need to check the two conditions above. \n\n- Let's pick two arbitrary vectors in $\\Pi,$ namely, ![](images/da/da46ac38012b220c5cf956076a81b76a21eb1242d21fae69f071531401d5d41d.svg) and ![](images/3e/3ee33188044335cd84f42b7621a5d255fdab972d89bef3d816c2b5a4da93dab0.svg) If we compute their sum, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u+v & =[\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} \\\\ {u}_{2} \\\\ 4{u}_{1}-2{u}_{2}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{2} \\\\ 4{v}_{1}-2{v}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}+{v}_{1} \\\\ {u}_{2}+{v}_{2} \\\\ 4{u}_{1}-2{u}_{2}+4{v}_{1}-2{v}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}+{v}_{1} \\\\ {u}_{2}+{v}_{2} \\\\ 4\\cdot ({u}_{1}+{v}_{1})-2\\cdot ({u}_{2}+{v}_{2})\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the condition $z=4x-2y.$ Therefore, $\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v}\\in \\Pi. \\quad {\\color{green}\\checkmark}$\n\n- Let's pick an arbitrary vector in $\\Pi,$ say, ![](images/27/277ea18a8e1721fdaa880025e3abb4fcb1ff3e8ec96730ca7597fbd584ef6500.svg) Then, for any scalar $c$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c\\cdot u & =c\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} \\\\ {u}_{2} \\\\ 4{u}_{1}-2{u}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}c{u}_{1} \\\\ c{u}_{2} \\\\ 4\\cdot c{u}_{1}-2\\cdot c{u}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the condition $z=4x-2y.$ Therefore, $c\\cdot \\mathbf{u}\\in \\Pi. \\quad {\\color{green}\\checkmark}$\n\nThe set $\\Pi$ satisfies both conditions. So, we can conclude that it is a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^3.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "3fcbfc6c-e5f6-5344-8ddd-779b625a03ea" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6860 :tutorial/title "Planes as Subspace of Three-Dimensional Space"} :step/id #uuid "2d8c8e20-696b-5df3-beaf-a78962ce0420" :step/math-academy-id 41675 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120887}}] :activity/title "Subspaces of N-Dimensional Space" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186120047}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417223} :activity/id #uuid "9b35dfb4-fca3-545e-9624-8422a0c70703" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051046 :topic/id #uuid "7cf616eb-2b54-52d2-8407-1b2fc912b2d9" :topic/math-academy-id 1853} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417220 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275393 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "97742b8a-34cf-5189-af20-df43023c61dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464494} {:answer/id #uuid "7ae0d77e-ad6b-5f6a-9374-9d4e224b4416" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464495}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186464494} :answer-field/id #uuid "744a436e-d533-5945-91e9-1188f48f53b0" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465181} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16e608cd-a84d-5934-9295-cbb3b7804914" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464496} {:answer/id #uuid "5eddee3a-9355-5dc5-9b2b-9982d99d11a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464497}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186464496} :answer-field/id #uuid "1c6756c7-064e-5db1-88b0-027f7aa281d0" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465182} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "587ce5fe-38e0-52b8-9ed9-30568644f99a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464498} {:answer/id #uuid "6fc16eec-a5c1-509c-b0b2-1e2793716ac0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464499}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186464498} :answer-field/id #uuid "b9b26f90-c8d9-55db-9b15-dcac52fe238a" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465183} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "feacad16-16da-5992-b7ff-e20c8092b4be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464500} {:answer/id #uuid "dda3dadb-3a83-5c87-9790-b31896571ae2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464501}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186464500} :answer-field/id #uuid "c0debc5b-2d21-5008-aa37-bbbd1df242a4" :answer-field/key "IV" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465184}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "84592f87-8902-5e5c-972a-3aba1b1c18c1" :question/math-academy-id "q-157377" :question/problem "Let $L$ be the line in ${R}^{3}$ that passes through the points $(1,1,1)$ and $(2,-1,2).$ Which of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** $L$ contains the zero-vector\n- **II.** $L$ is closed under vector addition\n- **III.** $L$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- **IV.** $L$ is a subspace of ${R}^{3}$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is false. The direction vector of the line $L$ is \n\n$$\nd=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThis means that the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor $t\\in R,$ and the corresponding Cartesian equation is \n\n$$\nx-1=\\frac{y-1}{-2}=z-1.\n$$\n\nBut this line does not pass through the origin since \n\n$$\n(0)-1\\ne \\frac{(0)-1}{-2}\\ne (0)-1.\\,\\times \n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Let's consider the vectors $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ from $L$ (the position vectors of the given points). Then,\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nHowever, the point $(3,0,3)$ does not belong to $L$ since\n\n$$\n(3)-1\\ne \\frac{(0)-1}{-2}\\ne (3)-1.\\,\\times \n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $0\\notin L,$ this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since statements II and III are false, $L$ is *not* a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\""} :step/id #uuid "1436249c-5de8-51a2-9062-17b0c4a22818" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417224}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417221 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275391 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "944e44ff-e533-5ed4-96bb-5a535eaddc00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464478} {:answer/id #uuid "d9a34893-198a-5797-a5eb-2dac1413854e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464479}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186464479} :answer-field/id #uuid "0b789df4-0f0f-56cb-9ecd-1817894caa49" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465173} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10e4fe65-09fc-52db-9ad9-c4b810d95943" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464480} {:answer/id #uuid "a57fa40c-c577-5110-ad32-33826b9d5ffa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464481}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186464481} :answer-field/id #uuid "e97038ed-fec6-54a7-be13-57daf6ed6250" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465174} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9530189a-66d7-5ec1-bcdd-d8a159b2bb43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464482} {:answer/id #uuid "5ecdaacb-8609-5a2d-aeb6-96682093c1e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464483}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186464483} :answer-field/id #uuid "0d5022e7-1fa9-59b1-bb96-9869737b531f" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465175} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f94ae52d-af32-5aa4-b47f-703bd283b958" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464484} {:answer/id #uuid "def3d7d0-1890-579f-af10-3e07494b2e5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464485}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186464485} :answer-field/id #uuid "627aa078-61dc-5bc5-a57c-68e48233cb15" :answer-field/key "IV" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465176}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f036c2d4-285c-5c5e-a300-6732346f17c0" :question/math-academy-id "q-157374" :question/problem "Let $L$ be the line in ${R}^{3}$ that passes through the points $(0,0,0)$ and $(1,-2,3).$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $L$ contains the zero-vector\n\n- **II.** $L$ is closed under vector addition\n\n- **III.** $L$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n\n- **IV.** $L$ is a subspace of ${R}^{3}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nFirst, note that the direction vector of the line $L$ is \n\n$$\nd=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThis means that the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ -2t \\\\ 3t\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor $t\\in R,$ and the corresponding Cartesian equation is \n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{y}{2}=\\frac{z}{3}.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, from the definition of $L$ we have that the point $(0,0,0)$ is in $L.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Let, for example, $a=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ -2a \\\\ 3a\\end{aligned}]\\in L$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}b \\\\ -2b \\\\ 3b\\end{aligned}]\\in L.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ -2a \\\\ 3a\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}b \\\\ -2b \\\\ 3b\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}a+b \\\\ (-2a)+(-2b) \\\\ 3a+3b\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}a+b \\\\ -2(a+b) \\\\ 3(a+b)\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the conditions $x=-\\frac{y}{2}=\\frac{z}{3}.$ Therefore, $a+b\\in L.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Let, for example, $a=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ -2a \\\\ 3a\\end{aligned}]\\in L$ and $k\\in R.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k\\cdot a & =k\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ -2a \\\\ 3a\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}ka \\\\ k(-2a) \\\\ k(3a)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}ka \\\\ -2(ka) \\\\ 3(ka)\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the conditions $x=-\\frac{y}{2}=\\frac{z}{3}.$ Therefore, $k\\cdot a\\in L.$\n\n- Statement IV is true. Since statements II and III are true, $L$ is a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"All the statements are true.\""} :step/id #uuid "1821a5bc-a723-5459-b893-12f5b2f84d53" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417220}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417222 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120152 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d725758f-8f09-58e8-a2b8-2c73f5b76c7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448438} {:answer/id #uuid "d4673597-a503-59f4-ba36-5ea8d99378be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448439}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186448438} :answer-field/id #uuid "99cc9849-b09a-5b7e-b6bd-df791e15e7f1" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449259} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "576125d5-2f3a-54f6-873a-edf7ed73af90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448440} {:answer/id #uuid "2e90c1f6-06f7-5cdb-bc41-44f405c2b307" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448441}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186448440} :answer-field/id #uuid "66c9ad16-74a3-59d5-82ce-29f0a17d9692" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449260} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bc3fc09c-bafe-5d89-864c-a4e4068a7ab0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448442} {:answer/id #uuid "3d8ec534-2e8c-56f5-8378-9653827802a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448443}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186448442} :answer-field/id #uuid "1faedf87-7dbd-5413-975e-1106b28c7dac" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449261} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "502ad113-cb99-5261-ad89-7832aa772263" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448444} {:answer/id #uuid "64ef3e1e-82e1-5684-9094-80783b5e97ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448445}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186448445} :answer-field/id #uuid "5b3216b8-68d0-5976-8b72-88a30238ee44" :answer-field/key "IV" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449262}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "4f59c175-a7f1-58ec-b16f-fd24b9c9dd63" :question/math-academy-id "q-156685" :question/problem "Let $Π$ be the plane with equation $x-y-z=1$ in ${R}^{3}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $Π$ contains the zero-vector\n- **II.** $Π$ is closed under vector addition\n- **III.** $Π$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- **IV.** $Π$ is **not** a subspace of ${R}^{3}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nNotice that $Π$ contains vectors of the form \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ x-y-1\\end{aligned}],\\,x,y\\in R.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is false. The line does not pass through the origin since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =x-y-1 \\\\ (0) & =(0)-(0)-(1) \\\\ 0 & =-1\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Let's consider $a=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\in Π$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\in Π.$ Then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0+1 \\\\ 0+0 \\\\ -1+0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ does not belong to $Π$ since \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =x-y-1 \\\\ (-1) & =(1)-(0)-1 \\\\ -1 & =0\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $0\\notin Π,$ this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement IV is true. Since statements II and III are false, $L$ is *not* a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\""} :step/id #uuid "647ef6bb-9fcf-5b00-95cb-b3da5691fc0c" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417221}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417223 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417219 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7384a9a6-a6f3-509e-8604-32e6ff1cf91f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453416} {:answer/id #uuid "d926c783-6a31-5aad-8676-68e3ed986684" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453417}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186453417} :answer-field/id #uuid "a16f14e7-1180-5cad-8b96-837d1cc5647f" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454458} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f86d39fb-1e90-5901-85fd-3a8ced53e62f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453418} {:answer/id #uuid "a4e62c65-eae5-5cc0-b334-901e889e9707" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453419}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186453419} :answer-field/id #uuid "3acee59e-520e-5e8f-8529-6999b135978a" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454459} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "31cc429d-07c8-5275-a610-638d58689d91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453420} {:answer/id #uuid "8469e1a3-49e3-5d1c-a349-3f76f08bd629" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453421}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186453421} :answer-field/id #uuid "43a7e42a-5e2f-5d59-ab7e-86b6db1851a4" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454460} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "332b4d60-25b6-5bcb-b419-6cb43732aabb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453422} {:answer/id #uuid "e402ecbd-276a-5177-b646-7e13763d957b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453423}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186453422} :answer-field/id #uuid "eab3e9d9-7d15-5b97-a15c-d97464e94366" :answer-field/key "IV" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454461}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "feee3179-e7d1-55ab-bcac-f334cca10495" :question/math-academy-id "q-156620" :question/problem "Let $L$ be the line with equation $4x=2y$ in ${R}^{2}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $L$ contains the zero-vector\n- **II.** $L$ is closed under vector addition\n- **III.** $L$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- **IV.** $L$ is **not** a subspace of ${R}^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nNotice that $L$ contains vectors of the form \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ 2x\\end{aligned}],\\,x\\in R.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is true. The coordinates of the zero-vector satisfy the equation $4x=2y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x & =2y \\\\ 4(0) & =2(0) \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Let, for example, $a=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 2a\\end{aligned}]\\in L$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}b \\\\ 2b\\end{aligned}]\\in L.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 2a\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}b \\\\ 2b\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}a+b \\\\ 2a+2b\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}a+b \\\\ 2(a+b)\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the condition $4x=2y.$ Therefore, $a+b\\in L.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Let, for example, $a=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 2a\\end{aligned}]\\in L$ and $k\\in R.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k\\cdot a & =k\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 2a\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}ka \\\\ k(2a)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}ka \\\\ 2(ka)\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhich satisfies the condition $4x=2y.$ Therefore, $k\\cdot a\\in L.$\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since statements II and III are true, $L$ must be a subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "a6b1b4f4-42fd-518f-9611-64b9f9fd951d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417222}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417224 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275399 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7e283f24-c3a1-59cf-9e3c-55ce9bf0a94a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464526} {:answer/id #uuid "62297675-ca20-5ed1-a25c-956c7f98abe1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464527}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186464526} :answer-field/id #uuid "febd412b-208a-57f4-a3e5-07cc980e96ea" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465197} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c2929686-a438-5316-b4de-d0b3d0e5e45b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464528} {:answer/id #uuid "f37d4317-a9ec-5c5a-9f0b-ed6e65458994" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464529}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186464528} :answer-field/id #uuid "9702f944-1db8-518a-acb2-526219576d0e" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465198} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6be4fdbb-c7e4-5655-b045-b6f33961203b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464530} {:answer/id #uuid "344f3967-9d4d-5472-83ad-054c661bb257" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464531}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186464530} :answer-field/id #uuid "70cbf976-fe8d-5ca6-b921-4a95b4f265e8" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465199} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3f902f17-2143-5f18-a934-cec9caf9ce4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464532} {:answer/id #uuid "c348bb49-51ad-5388-bcca-dcb41c8a20bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464533}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186464532} :answer-field/id #uuid "0e36ef1a-6a50-5ba0-b80b-aa2e41f943fa" :answer-field/key "IV" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465200}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "99eda82b-48bc-5827-9c44-f2725d46a5d4" :question/math-academy-id "q-157476" :question/problem "Let $Π$ be the plane with equation $x-2y+2z=3$ in ${R}^{3}.$ Which of the following statements are true? \n\nI. $Π$ contains the zero-vector\n\nII. $Π$ is closed under vector addition\n\nIII. $Π$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n\nIV. $Π$ is a subspace of ${R}^{3}$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nNotice that $Π$ contains vectors of the form \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3+2y-2z \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,y,z\\in R.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is false. The line does not pass through the origin since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-2y+2z & =3 \\\\ (0)-2(0)+2(0) & =3 \\\\ 0 & =3\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Let's consider $a=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\in Π$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\in Π.$ Then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3+1 \\\\ 0+0 \\\\ 0+1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, $[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ does not belong to $Π$ since \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-2y+2z & =3 \\\\ (4)-2(0)+2(1) & =3 \\\\ 6 & =3\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $0\\notin Π,$ this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since statements II and III are false, $L$ is *not* a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\""} :step/id #uuid "f20bfc1c-6733-50b5-9d02-7dbeed933bee"}] :activity/title "Subspaces of N-Dimensional Space" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417212}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489339} :activity/id #uuid "05594f5b-c67a-5e94-adc0-9f23070bfc99" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051046 :topic/id #uuid "7cf616eb-2b54-52d2-8407-1b2fc912b2d9" :topic/math-academy-id 1853} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489304 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489173 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d6e4eb6-6d41-5ba5-b742-40b17f8d54e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488532} {:answer/id #uuid "3c3fab0d-b7ef-53be-8720-40063138c092" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488594} {:answer/id #uuid "759d092c-bea5-5b3e-a194-100cae11ae72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488703} {:answer/id #uuid "8d4caa60-0479-5815-b2a0-47647bc7550b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "All the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488753} {:answer/id #uuid "d7a005c0-68dc-5657-b99c-da3dd9395ea0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488915}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488753} :answer-field/id #uuid "ecd11032-a320-56b0-8408-67c72e266d26" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489154}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "00c50921-42ae-53d0-a1bc-1a992257ffe3" :question/math-academy-id "q-157467" :question/problem "Let $Π$ be the plane with equation $z=3x+2y$ in ${R}^{3}.$ Which of the following statements are true? \n\nI. $Π$ contains the zero-vector\n\nII. $Π$ is closed under vector addition\n\nIII. $Π$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n\nIV. $Π$ is a subspace of ${R}^{3}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nNotice that $Π$ contains vectors of the form \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ 3x+2y\\end{aligned}],\\,x,y\\in R.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is true. The coordinates of the zero-vector satisfy the equation $z=3x+2y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =3x+2y \\\\ (0) & =3(0)+2(0) \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Let, for example, $a=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2} \\\\ 3{a}_{1}+2{a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\\in Π$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{2} \\\\ 3{b}_{1}+2{b}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\\in Π.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2} \\\\ 3{a}_{1}+2{a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{2} \\\\ 3{b}_{1}+2{b}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1}+{b}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}+{b}_{2} \\\\ (3{a}_{1}+2{a}_{2})+(3{b}_{1}+2{b}_{2})\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1}+{b}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}+{b}_{2} \\\\ 3({a}_{1}+{b}_{1})+2({a}_{2}+{b}_{2})\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the condition $z=3x+2y.$ Therefore, $a+b\\in Π.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Let, for example, $a=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2} \\\\ 3{a}_{1}+2{a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\\in Π$ and $k\\in R.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k\\cdot a & =k\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2} \\\\ 3{a}_{1}+2{a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}k{a}_{1} \\\\ k{a}_{2} \\\\ k(3{a}_{1}+2{a}_{2})\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}k{a}_{1} \\\\ k{a}_{2} \\\\ 3k{a}_{1}+2k{a}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the condition $z=3x+2y.$ Therefore, $k\\cdot a\\in Π.$\n\n- Statement IV is true. Since statements II and III are true, the plane $Π$ must be a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"All the statements are true.\""} :step/id #uuid "21466245-f430-5f32-bab1-95d0310fd556"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489318 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120153 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e25ac228-d193-5ebc-8755-db62b8ed2d6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448446} {:answer/id #uuid "97e87930-b43d-5bac-9e1c-47a721b48491" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448447}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186448447} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d500936-ee82-5080-ac08-bef3d3eaa3c1" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449263} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "86c8192b-16cf-5e19-8b80-1add681eb5aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448448} {:answer/id #uuid "cd6afe2d-18b1-5a6d-ac8f-bec0627bec1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448449}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186448449} :answer-field/id #uuid "2c9a0b63-a428-59e7-8e61-153351ae6756" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449264} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "abdcf3ff-572a-5007-97fb-7924447d2418" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448450} {:answer/id #uuid "7d29026a-1829-5065-a9e8-5223d4001976" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448451}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186448451} :answer-field/id #uuid "5735db43-5ab0-59bd-bf1d-6365653e745a" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449265} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e99e80e5-f93b-5878-83fd-8192fe31da43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448452} {:answer/id #uuid "6bb90d13-99f5-5226-86da-aa3c3e18067c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448453}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186448452} :answer-field/id #uuid "7a9210ac-f65d-536c-92f2-e1ec02b3e035" :answer-field/key "IV" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449266}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ceae9baf-b635-5e21-b116-a9e3e8cfea9c" :question/math-academy-id "q-156719" :question/problem "Let $Π$ be the plane with equation $z=x-y$ in ${R}^{3}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $Π$ contains the zero-vector\n- **II.** $Π$ is closed under vector addition\n- **III.** $Π$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- **IV.** $Π$ is **not** a subspace of ${R}^{3}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nNotice that $Π$ contains vectors of the form \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ x-y\\end{aligned}],\\,x,y\\in R.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is true. The coordinates of the zero-vector satisfy the equation $z=x-y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =x-y \\\\ (0) & =(0)-(0) \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Let, for example, $a=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2} \\\\ {a}_{1}-{a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\\in Π$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{2} \\\\ {b}_{1}-{b}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\\in Π.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2} \\\\ {a}_{1}-{a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{2} \\\\ {b}_{1}+{b}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1}+{b}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}+{b}_{2} \\\\ ({a}_{1}-{a}_{2})+({b}_{1}-{b}_{2})\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1}+{b}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}+{b}_{2} \\\\ ({a}_{1}+{b}_{1})-({a}_{2}+{b}_{2})\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the condition $z=x-y.$ Therefore, $a+b\\in Π.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Let, for example, $a=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2} \\\\ {a}_{1}-{a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\\in Π$ and $k\\in R.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k\\cdot a & =k\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2} \\\\ {a}_{1}-{a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}k{a}_{1} \\\\ k{a}_{2} \\\\ k({a}_{1}-{a}_{2})\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}k{a}_{1} \\\\ k{a}_{2} \\\\ k{a}_{1}-k{a}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the condition $z=x-y.$ Therefore, $k\\cdot a\\in Π.$\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since statements II and III are true, $Π$ must be a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "50364cf2-ba89-551c-8162-eed479c1cceb" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489346}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489320 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275396 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5c4e438f-df53-5f70-8e5d-7a169145d53c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464518} {:answer/id #uuid "af822d08-9b9b-57a7-a8c8-33883456d16c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464519}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186464519} :answer-field/id #uuid "fb16460b-15cc-5a45-acb1-fc84c3e266eb" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465193} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c02e032e-2b5a-5921-b21e-d772b83f8b2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464520} {:answer/id #uuid "7b351d6f-31f6-5d4a-b904-75ae0914c6d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464521}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186464521} :answer-field/id #uuid "31556c67-692f-5001-8e1d-96f75b44588a" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465194} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d1287fcc-3fcb-510a-b75c-41ef53051154" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464522} {:answer/id #uuid "b3ab104e-08b0-5836-a2c0-cb9cfa04791d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464523}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186464523} :answer-field/id #uuid "f71fabb8-3089-5b76-9692-7fbacfd573c7" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465195} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "55db86b2-c488-5f67-9c04-0a4b0afc906c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464524} {:answer/id #uuid "de642d2b-0e51-5ff8-9f66-d4e26ea5ccdf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464525}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186464525} :answer-field/id #uuid "06fe3143-439a-5767-94b9-14c70cde2a7d" :answer-field/key "IV" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465196}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "19a5b6b6-a775-5cf8-81ee-dfec54297bbf" :question/math-academy-id "q-157464" :question/problem "Let $Π$ be the plane with equation $z=x+2y$ in ${R}^{3}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $Π$ contains the zero-vector\n\n- **II.** $Π$ is closed under vector addition\n\n- **III.** $Π$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n\n- **IV.** $Π$ is a subspace of ${R}^{3}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nNotice that $Π$ contains vectors of the form \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ x+2y\\end{aligned}],\\,x,y\\in R.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is true. The coordinates of the zero-vector satisfy the equation $z=x+2y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =x+2y \\\\ (0) & =(0)+2(0) \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Let, for example, $a=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2} \\\\ {a}_{1}+2{a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\\in Π$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{2} \\\\ {b}_{1}+2{b}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\\in Π.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2} \\\\ {a}_{1}+2{a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{2} \\\\ {b}_{1}+2{b}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1}+{b}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}+{b}_{2} \\\\ ({a}_{1}+2{a}_{2})+({b}_{1}+2{b}_{2})\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1}+{b}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}+{b}_{2} \\\\ ({a}_{1}+{b}_{1})+2({a}_{2}+{b}_{2})\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the condition $z=x+2y.$ Therefore, $a+b\\in Π.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Let, for example, $a=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2} \\\\ {a}_{1}+2{a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\\in Π$ and $k\\in R.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k\\cdot a & =k\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2} \\\\ {a}_{1}+2{a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}k{a}_{1} \\\\ k{a}_{2} \\\\ k({a}_{1}+2{a}_{2})\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}k{a}_{1} \\\\ k{a}_{2} \\\\ k{a}_{1}+2(k{a}_{2})\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the condition $z=x+2y.$ Therefore, $k\\cdot a\\in Π.$\n\n- Statement IV is true. Since statements II and III are true, the plane $Π$ must be a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"All the statements are true.\""} :step/id #uuid "562229c5-8d32-5b9a-8f54-0759c5f5092c" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489304}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489339 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489255 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5630f304-d35f-5326-b26c-3feb0d15ab6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488634} {:answer/id #uuid "5f19d8a6-dfab-5436-b75e-578f5c45a2f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "All the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488652} {:answer/id #uuid "8963240b-c71f-5fe4-9d91-80374eefb00a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488744} {:answer/id #uuid "b00e08ba-08b5-5691-9161-9a7d57702f37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488821} {:answer/id #uuid "d0dc412d-2d7f-5a7d-9147-f6af3bd08779" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488895}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488821} :answer-field/id #uuid "af22d48e-5724-584d-b3a5-2b9d70fcf5d0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489112}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a5bfe454-c84f-5da8-9381-6959e5f62f7a" :question/math-academy-id "q-156680" :question/problem "Let $L$ be the line in ${R}^{3}$ that passes through the points $(1,-1,2)$ and $(-1,1,1).$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $L$ contains the zero-vector\n\nII. $L$ is closed under vector addition\n\nIII. $L$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n\nIV. $L$ is **not** a subspace of ${R}^{3}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is false. The direction vector of the line $L$ is \n\n$$\nd=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThis means that the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor $t\\in R,$ and the corresponding Cartesian equation is \n\n$$\n\\frac{x-1}{-2}=\\frac{y+1}{2}=\\frac{z-2}{-1}.\n$$\n\nBut this line does not pass through the origin since \n\n$$\n\\frac{(0)-1}{-2}=\\frac{(0)+1}{2}\\ne \\frac{(0)-2}{-1}\\,\\times \n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Let's consider the vectors $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ from $L$ (the position vectors of the given points). Then,\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nHowever, the point $(0,0,3)$ does not belong to $L$ since\n\n$$\n\\frac{(0)-1}{-2}=\\frac{(0)+1}{2}\\ne -(3)+2.\\,\\times \n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $0\\notin L,$ this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement IV is true. Since statements II and III are false, $L$ is *not* a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\""} :step/id #uuid "8ef378a7-58e1-50e8-86be-81d99657fd29" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489318}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489346 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275390 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "080b3e48-0b4d-5884-a749-d0c69743c036" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464470} {:answer/id #uuid "0fdc165a-692f-5e31-a89e-08b14f3f236d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464471}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186464471} :answer-field/id #uuid "6561ebee-dcc5-5a26-ab24-3c9371a4f105" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465169} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "66eba07c-1747-5a4e-8e6c-4f1bf6577c6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464472} {:answer/id #uuid "3bf0d016-9043-5e90-b774-a176c0b9864f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464473}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186464473} :answer-field/id #uuid "d9cfe2e7-684f-559b-9bb4-b5d0e7038c84" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465170} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1908d05a-25a0-5d8a-ac1d-bd65dd44be1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464474} {:answer/id #uuid "7f230175-96a5-5648-a72b-a3f53ab5bf0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464475}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186464475} :answer-field/id #uuid "97b76e0e-f96a-590c-afb1-fad6d6e43575" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465171} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3cd5189d-1f84-59b4-aad4-6cd6be89149c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464476} {:answer/id #uuid "58c78ec7-38c7-545a-9b3d-b3b31b558b7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464477}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186464476} :answer-field/id #uuid "1f295196-509c-5417-828b-b1db47be1605" :answer-field/key "IV" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465172}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "712144d5-1b91-55e7-970d-beb256e92d9d" :question/math-academy-id "q-157300" :question/problem "Let $L$ be the line in ${R}^{3}$ that passes through the points $(0,0,0)$ and $(2,1,-1).$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $L$ contains the zero-vector\n\n- **II.** $L$ is closed under vector addition\n\n- **III.** $L$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n\n- **IV.** $L$ is **not** a subspace of ${R}^{3}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nFirst, note that the direction vector of the line $L$ is \n\n$$\nd=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThis means that the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2t \\\\ t \\\\ -t\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor $t\\in R,$ and the corresponding Cartesian equation is \n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{2}=y=-z.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, from the definition of $L$ we have that the point $(0,0,0)$ is in $L.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Let, for example, $a=[\\begin{aligned}2a \\\\ a \\\\ -a\\end{aligned}]\\in L$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}2b \\\\ b \\\\ -b\\end{aligned}]\\in L.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =[\\begin{aligned}2a \\\\ a \\\\ -a\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2b \\\\ b \\\\ -b\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2a+2b \\\\ a+b \\\\ (-a)+(-b)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2(a+b) \\\\ a+b \\\\ -(a+b)\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the conditions $\\frac{x}{2}=y=-z.$ Therefore, $a+b\\in L.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Let, for example, $a=[\\begin{aligned}2a \\\\ a \\\\ -a\\end{aligned}]\\in L$ and $k\\in R.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k\\cdot a & =k\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2a \\\\ a \\\\ -a\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}k(2a) \\\\ ka \\\\ k(-a)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2(ka) \\\\ ka \\\\ -(ka)\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the conditions $\\frac{x}{2}=y=-z.$ Therefore, $k\\cdot a\\in L.$\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since statements II and III are true, $L$ is a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "9e51c793-12d9-5aac-a409-0a574ae80bab" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489320}}] :activity/title "Subspaces of N-Dimensional Space" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488450}]}
